WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.867 --> 00:00:02.035
- About three weeks ago I said

00:00:02.035 --> 00:00:04.604 align:start size:84% position:16%
you'll know that
there's some tension,

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:07.507
or a little bit of elbow room,

00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:10.176 align:start size:84% position:16%
when you start seeing
background quotes

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:13.113
from somebody close
to the White House,

00:00:13.113 --> 00:00:14.681
or within the White House,

00:00:14.681 --> 00:00:18.618 align:start position:0%
who says something about Kamala
Harris that would make it

00:00:18.618 --> 00:00:21.654
clear that she is
the Vice President.

00:00:21.654 --> 00:00:22.589
- [Dave] Mhm.

00:00:22.589 --> 00:00:23.857
- And so when I saw that,

00:00:23.857 --> 00:00:25.558
I'm like, ooh. To me,
that was kind of like,

00:00:25.558 --> 00:00:28.795 align:start size:88% position:13%
it was kind of the
first time you had seen

00:00:28.795 --> 00:00:30.730
a little bit of a
brush back pitch,

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:31.831 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I don't know what's
going on internally

00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:33.500
because you'd never see anybody.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.169
They don't talk, they
don't do anything.

00:00:36.169 --> 00:00:39.773 align:start size:97% position:3%
But that was definitely a
little bit of a just remember.

00:00:39.773 --> 00:00:42.042 align:start size:84% position:16%
And also, if I
were Kamala Harris,

00:00:42.042 --> 00:00:42.876
I'd be mad.

00:00:42.876 --> 00:00:45.412
(upbeat music)

00:00:50.083 --> 00:00:51.317
- I'm Dave Rubin

00:00:51.317 --> 00:00:53.586 line:0%
and joining me today
is a former White House

00:00:53.586 --> 00:00:55.822 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
press secretary
to George W. Bush,

00:00:55.822 --> 00:00:58.191 line:0%
co-anchor of America's Newsroom,

00:00:58.191 --> 00:01:01.394
and cohost of The
Five on Fox News,

00:01:01.394 --> 00:01:04.864
and author of the new book,
Everything Will Be Okay,

00:01:04.864 --> 00:01:07.367
Life lessons for young women

00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:09.536
from a former young woman.

00:01:09.536 --> 00:01:11.604 align:start size:94% position:6%
Dana Perino, welcome
back to The Rubin Report.

00:01:11.604 --> 00:01:14.240
- Honored to be here,
thanks for having me.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.176
- You are a busy lady,

00:01:16.176 --> 00:01:19.813
and before we get to
any of that busy stuff,

00:01:19.813 --> 00:01:22.849
most importantly, how
is your dog Jasper?

00:01:22.849 --> 00:01:26.052
I could do just an hour on
dogs and Jasper with you.

00:01:26.052 --> 00:01:26.986
How's he doing?

00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:28.221
- It's actually one of the ways

00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:29.889 align:start size:84% position:16%
that you and I first
connected, right?

00:01:29.889 --> 00:01:31.124
It was dogs,

00:01:31.124 --> 00:01:33.793 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I see that great
picture of Clyde.

00:01:33.793 --> 00:01:35.295
Is that Clyde there in
the picture behind you?

00:01:35.295 --> 00:01:36.830 align:start size:88% position:13%
- That's that's actually
Emma over there--

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:37.897
- [Dana] That was Emma.

00:01:37.897 --> 00:01:39.132
Okay, yep.

00:01:39.132 --> 00:01:41.734 align:start size:81% position:19%
- But they look
bizarrely similar,

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:42.969
which is a total coincidence.

00:01:42.969 --> 00:01:44.704
- I remember when Clyde
came into your life,

00:01:44.704 --> 00:01:45.705
it was like, wait,

00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:47.240
this is perfect.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:48.975 align:start size:97% position:3%
You weren't anticipating,
and you weren't planning it,

00:01:48.975 --> 00:01:50.176
but it all worked out great.

00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:52.145
So Jasper's great.

00:01:52.145 --> 00:01:55.849 line:0%
He is going to be nine in April.

00:01:55.849 --> 00:01:59.586 line:0%
In late January, he came out
of the bedroom one morning,

00:01:59.586 --> 00:02:01.254
and was walking real strange,

00:02:01.254 --> 00:02:02.789
tail down, hair up.

00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:05.191
Obviously you know your dog,
you know something's wrong,

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:08.361 align:start size:97% position:3%
but unfortunately they
can't tell you what's wrong.

00:02:08.361 --> 00:02:10.530
So we took him to the vet.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:11.898
They did a check.

00:02:11.898 --> 00:02:14.601
There was a small tumor,

00:02:14.601 --> 00:02:17.871
a malignant tumor, on
his small intestine.

00:02:17.871 --> 00:02:19.472
They removed that
through surgery

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:21.541 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then we've
waited quite a while

00:02:21.541 --> 00:02:24.677
to find out what the
biopsy results were,

00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:26.613 align:start size:84% position:16%
because they said
it was either a very

00:02:26.613 --> 00:02:29.249 align:start size:84% position:16%
common malignant tumor
or a very rare one.

00:02:29.249 --> 00:02:30.950
If it was a very rare one,

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:33.820 align:start size:94% position:6%
that was going to be very
tough news, bad news.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:35.955
But it turned out it
was the common one.

00:02:35.955 --> 00:02:37.423
The surgery was successful,

00:02:37.423 --> 00:02:39.058 align:start size:84% position:16%
and we're just going
to wait and see

00:02:39.058 --> 00:02:40.326
what the oncologist says,

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:42.028
if there's anything
else we need to do.

00:02:42.028 --> 00:02:46.065
But judging by the way that
these tumors usually go,

00:02:46.065 --> 00:02:47.567
he should be fine.

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:51.171
And I would say about 12
days after the surgery,

00:02:51.171 --> 00:02:53.339 align:start size:84% position:16%
he made this
remarkable turnaround,

00:02:53.339 --> 00:02:56.142 align:start position:0%
and all of a sudden he was back
to being his very athletic,

00:02:56.142 --> 00:02:58.077
affectionate, silly self,

00:02:58.077 --> 00:03:00.146
and so we're thrilled.

00:03:00.146 --> 00:03:01.481
Thanks for asking.

00:03:01.481 --> 00:03:02.982
- Yeah.

00:03:02.982 --> 00:03:05.151
Do you ever find that when
you post pictures of Jasper,

00:03:05.151 --> 00:03:06.686 align:start size:81% position:19%
or just any of the
personal stuff,

00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:10.089 align:start size:97% position:3%
that in a way people care a
lot more about that than just,

00:03:10.089 --> 00:03:11.291
sort of, you know,

00:03:11.291 --> 00:03:13.593 align:start size:84% position:16%
what Dana might
think about Congress

00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:15.728
voted two votes extra this way,

00:03:15.728 --> 00:03:17.063
that way, the other thing.

00:03:17.063 --> 00:03:18.898
Like, there is some
other connection,

00:03:18.898 --> 00:03:20.099
and I think this is in some ways

00:03:20.099 --> 00:03:21.334
partly what the book's about.

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:23.469
Like, that people
want something else

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:26.139 align:start size:88% position:13%
beyond just the
bludgeoning of politics.

00:03:26.139 --> 00:03:27.740
- Yeah, and one of
the things that I,

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:29.676
you know, I have a rule.

00:03:29.676 --> 00:03:31.444
No politics at the dog park.

00:03:33.313 --> 00:03:35.348
Animals, our love for them,

00:03:35.348 --> 00:03:37.183
our dominion over them,

00:03:38.318 --> 00:03:41.621
is something so special in life.

00:03:41.621 --> 00:03:43.089
And I grew up on a ranch.

00:03:43.089 --> 00:03:44.791
So all the animals were special,

00:03:44.791 --> 00:03:47.560 align:start size:97% position:3%
but the dogs and the
horses were really special.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.028 align:start size:84% position:16%
You have that
connection with them,

00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:51.030
that eye contact, that love.

00:03:52.232 --> 00:03:53.933
I guess some people feel
that way about cats too.

00:03:53.933 --> 00:03:55.535
Can't say I ever had
the same experience,

00:03:55.535 --> 00:03:57.637
though I grew up
with cats as well.

00:03:57.637 --> 00:03:58.871
- Yeah.

00:03:58.871 --> 00:04:00.139 align:start size:84% position:16%
- But I do find
that there is a way

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:01.307
to connect with people that,

00:04:01.307 --> 00:04:03.243 align:start size:84% position:16%
you know, like
Donna Brazil and I.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:05.745
We've been friends
for a long time,

00:04:05.745 --> 00:04:07.480
ever since hurricane Katrina.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:10.316 align:start size:88% position:13%
And she worked with
the Bush administration

00:04:10.316 --> 00:04:12.185
on restoring New Orleans,

00:04:12.185 --> 00:04:14.887 align:start size:84% position:16%
but we really bonded
over our dogs.

00:04:14.887 --> 00:04:18.224
And we both had different
dogs, our previous dogs,

00:04:18.224 --> 00:04:19.459
when we first knew each other.

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:21.227
Sort of like you
and me in a way.

00:04:21.227 --> 00:04:22.528
And then, you know,

00:04:22.528 --> 00:04:24.330
dogs, unfortunately they don't

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:25.665
live as long as we would like.

00:04:25.665 --> 00:04:27.567
And then the best way to heal

00:04:27.567 --> 00:04:30.103
your heart is to
get another dog.

00:04:30.103 --> 00:04:31.638
So I think it's been good,

00:04:31.638 --> 00:04:34.974
and also this year has been
awful for so many reasons,

00:04:34.974 --> 00:04:36.175
in terms of the pandemic,

00:04:36.175 --> 00:04:39.879 align:start size:84% position:16%
but the fact that many
families have now,

00:04:39.879 --> 00:04:43.483
or even single people, have
emptied the dog shelters,

00:04:43.483 --> 00:04:46.919
and adopted dogs, as
I know you have done,

00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:48.054
and advocate for adoption.

00:04:48.054 --> 00:04:50.056
And I'll tell you,

00:04:50.056 --> 00:04:55.061
I think that makes for
kinder people over time.

00:04:56.496 --> 00:04:57.664
- Yeah, I'm with you. I
mean, you know the story.

00:04:57.664 --> 00:04:58.898 align:start size:84% position:16%
I heard the lock
downs were happening,

00:04:58.898 --> 00:05:00.366
and they were going to
put all these dogs down.

00:05:00.366 --> 00:05:03.202 align:start size:94% position:6%
I ran to the shelter, they
had the things signed.

00:05:03.202 --> 00:05:04.671
Clyde was being put down.

00:05:04.671 --> 00:05:07.206
He was unnamed, unnamed dog
being put down that day,

00:05:07.206 --> 00:05:09.609
and we snagged him,
and he's doing okay.

00:05:09.609 --> 00:05:10.810
So before we get into the book,

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:12.145 align:start size:84% position:16%
let's talk a little
bit about just

00:05:12.145 --> 00:05:14.881 align:start size:84% position:16%
kind of what's going
on in the world.

00:05:16.015 --> 00:05:18.551 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's been a weird
year to say the least.

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:21.821
Well, actually,

00:05:21.821 --> 00:05:23.923 align:start size:97% position:3%
let's talk about sort of
your former job for a second,

00:05:23.923 --> 00:05:27.126 align:start size:84% position:16%
because press
secretary, Jen Psaki.

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:29.829
I've been giving her a bit
of a hard time on the show.

00:05:29.829 --> 00:05:33.499
She doesn't seem particularly
well equipped to do this job.

00:05:33.499 --> 00:05:35.802
She's always circling back.

00:05:35.802 --> 00:05:37.904
Lot of non answers.

00:05:37.904 --> 00:05:39.439
I suspect you've
got some sympathy--

00:05:39.439 --> 00:05:41.507
- Look, a non answer is an art.

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:44.243
- (laughs quickly) And she's
perfected that art I suppose.

00:05:44.243 --> 00:05:46.412
But I'm guessing you
have some sympathy.

00:05:46.412 --> 00:05:47.680
- I have a different view.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:51.551
Look, they've only been
in office for six weeks.

00:05:51.551 --> 00:05:53.086
There is a lot you don't know,

00:05:53.086 --> 00:05:56.222
and that's one of the reasons
you would want to circle back.

00:05:56.222 --> 00:05:57.690
She does come to the job with

00:05:57.690 --> 00:05:59.425 align:start size:84% position:16%
a lot more experience
than I had,

00:05:59.425 --> 00:06:01.361 align:start size:84% position:16%
because she was
the spokesperson at

00:06:01.361 --> 00:06:03.763
the State department
under President Obama.

00:06:03.763 --> 00:06:05.131
And that gave her two things.

00:06:05.131 --> 00:06:08.234 align:start size:84% position:16%
One, a deep dive
into foreign policy,

00:06:08.234 --> 00:06:10.636
which a lot of people
don't necessarily have,

00:06:10.636 --> 00:06:11.971
unless you've come
up through, you know,

00:06:11.971 --> 00:06:15.842
that industry or
that department.

00:06:15.842 --> 00:06:19.712 align:start size:94% position:6%
It takes a while to learn
all of those issues.

00:06:19.712 --> 00:06:22.448
And she used to get
a really hard time

00:06:22.448 --> 00:06:25.017 align:start size:84% position:16%
from a few reporters
in particular.

00:06:25.017 --> 00:06:27.754
James Rosen was one of
them, from Fox News,

00:06:27.754 --> 00:06:30.890
but Matt Lee of the
Associated Press,

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:35.895
he is an equal opportunity,
tough reporter on everybody.

00:06:37.029 --> 00:06:37.697 align:start size:84% position:16%
And they used to have
a lot of tangles.

00:06:37.697 --> 00:06:38.931
And remember,

00:06:38.931 --> 00:06:40.166 align:start size:84% position:16%
she also got caught
up in that whole thing

00:06:40.166 --> 00:06:42.502
of spying on James
Rosen and reporters.

00:06:42.502 --> 00:06:47.106
So she comes to the job with
a little bit more experience

00:06:47.106 --> 00:06:50.610 align:start size:88% position:13%
than many press
secretaries up to date.

00:06:52.111 --> 00:06:55.214
The other thing is is she has
a press corps that is one.

00:06:55.214 --> 00:06:57.717
There's not many
people in the room,

00:06:57.717 --> 00:07:00.153 align:start size:94% position:6%
because they are very
strict on COVID protocol.

00:07:00.153 --> 00:07:01.654
So there's probably, I think,

00:07:01.654 --> 00:07:04.424 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think there was like 10
reporters total in the room?

00:07:04.424 --> 00:07:06.893
That makes it for a
very different room.

00:07:06.893 --> 00:07:09.228
Masks also, I think,

00:07:09.228 --> 00:07:11.030
are a real barrier
to communication.

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:14.567 align:start size:88% position:13%
And even though somebody
might try to, like,

00:07:14.567 --> 00:07:16.135
hold their mask away, you know,

00:07:16.135 --> 00:07:17.470 align:start size:81% position:19%
there's this whole
thing going on

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:18.438
with you can't read lips,

00:07:18.438 --> 00:07:19.739
you can't read expressions.

00:07:19.739 --> 00:07:20.740
There's all of that.

00:07:21.707 --> 00:07:23.009
And the other thing is,

00:07:23.009 --> 00:07:24.510
I think President Biden is quite

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:27.380
happy not to drive the news.

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:28.214
And it's a weird thing,

00:07:28.214 --> 00:07:29.682
because I think, well,

00:07:29.682 --> 00:07:30.917
you have the opportunity.

00:07:30.917 --> 00:07:32.718
Why don't you drive the
news every single day?

00:07:32.718 --> 00:07:34.420
They seem to have this belief,

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:36.856
and we'll see how it
turns out for them,

00:07:36.856 --> 00:07:41.060
that they can do their
jobs in a very steady,

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:41.994
easy way.

00:07:41.994 --> 00:07:44.063
And I use that kind of phrasing

00:07:44.063 --> 00:07:46.799
because that feels like the
pace of the administration.

00:07:47.934 --> 00:07:51.003 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Is that partly just
because the media

00:07:51.003 --> 00:07:53.506
kind of likes them
perhaps a bit more

00:07:53.506 --> 00:07:55.508
than they liked
that Trump fella?

00:07:55.508 --> 00:07:56.742
So, you know,

00:07:56.742 --> 00:07:57.977
they're not going to
ride them as hard,

00:07:57.977 --> 00:07:59.312
it makes their job
a little bit easier.

00:07:59.312 --> 00:08:01.948
- I have a different
way of answering this.

00:08:01.948 --> 00:08:04.150
And I haven't thought
of this until just now,

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:05.418
because I haven't
really been asked.

00:08:05.418 --> 00:08:08.488
But if you think back to
the press secretaries,

00:08:09.856 --> 00:08:12.225 align:start size:97% position:3%
everyone kind of represents
their boss very well.

00:08:13.359 --> 00:08:14.293
Right?

00:08:14.293 --> 00:08:16.529
So think of Ari Fleischer,

00:08:16.529 --> 00:08:19.398
Tony Snow and me,
Scott McClellan.

00:08:20.766 --> 00:08:22.568
We had the confidence
of George W. Bush.

00:08:22.568 --> 00:08:23.836
You think of the
press secretaries,

00:08:23.836 --> 00:08:25.638
even though Sean Spicer was not

00:08:25.638 --> 00:08:27.173
press secretary for very long,

00:08:27.173 --> 00:08:28.574
he continues to be a part of

00:08:28.574 --> 00:08:31.043
President Trump's sort of orbit,

00:08:31.043 --> 00:08:32.078
I would say.

00:08:32.078 --> 00:08:33.312
The other press secretaries,

00:08:33.312 --> 00:08:34.847
Stephanie Grisham, a
little bit different.

00:08:34.847 --> 00:08:37.817
She never briefed, that might
be a little bit different one.

00:08:37.817 --> 00:08:42.021
But I think that Jen Psaki is
just kind of in the approach,

00:08:42.021 --> 00:08:46.025
tone, attitude of Joe Biden.

00:08:46.025 --> 00:08:51.030
And so maybe what you want
from a press secretary, one,

00:08:52.198 --> 00:08:53.799
you might want some
more answers, always,

00:08:53.799 --> 00:08:57.069 align:start size:94% position:6%
but you also to know
that what you are getting

00:08:57.069 --> 00:09:00.239
from that press secretary is
what the president is thinking,

00:09:00.239 --> 00:09:02.942
and how he thought about it,
how they got to that decision.

00:09:02.942 --> 00:09:06.445
I think that she's
actually doing that.

00:09:06.445 --> 00:09:07.713
- Yeah.

00:09:07.713 --> 00:09:09.949
Do you sense that
Biden is actually

00:09:09.949 --> 00:09:12.652
in charge of this operation?

00:09:12.652 --> 00:09:15.788 align:start size:88% position:13%
Because it doesn't
really seem like he is.

00:09:15.788 --> 00:09:17.523
- I mean, I hear
what you're saying.

00:09:17.523 --> 00:09:19.759
But I remember so many times,

00:09:19.759 --> 00:09:22.495 align:start size:88% position:13%
do you remember how
they said that Karl was,

00:09:22.495 --> 00:09:24.297
Karl Rove, was Bushes brain,

00:09:24.297 --> 00:09:26.098
or that really Dick
Cheney was really

00:09:26.098 --> 00:09:27.867
the one in charge of everything.

00:09:27.867 --> 00:09:29.268
It wasn't true.

00:09:29.268 --> 00:09:31.904
And I think about
this now, you know,

00:09:31.904 --> 00:09:34.240
the other day when
they did the attack

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:37.610
on the Iranian forces in Syria.

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:39.412 align:start size:84% position:16%
Did you see the news
that they didn't

00:09:39.412 --> 00:09:42.114
tell Kamala Harris until after?

00:09:42.114 --> 00:09:42.949
- Mhm.

00:09:42.949 --> 00:09:43.783
- So, to me,

00:09:43.783 --> 00:09:44.717
I think, yeah.

00:09:44.717 --> 00:09:46.586
I think that he is in charge.

00:09:47.687 --> 00:09:48.955
- Yeah.

00:09:48.955 --> 00:09:50.523
When you see something
like that get leaked,

00:09:50.523 --> 00:09:51.757
not that they did the attack,

00:09:51.757 --> 00:09:52.692 align:start size:88% position:13%
but that the Vice
President didn't know--

00:09:52.692 --> 00:09:53.859
- That they didn't tell her?

00:09:53.859 --> 00:09:54.860
- Yeah.

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:56.562
What do you make
of that process?

00:09:56.562 --> 00:09:58.497
Because it seems to me that
there's gotta be something

00:09:58.497 --> 00:10:00.399
going on there that
allows that to get,

00:10:00.399 --> 00:10:02.401 align:start size:84% position:16%
like, that's the
story in an odd way,

00:10:02.401 --> 00:10:03.336
more than, you know,

00:10:03.336 --> 00:10:04.503
bombing a random caravan.

00:10:04.503 --> 00:10:05.338
I think you're with me.

00:10:05.338 --> 00:10:06.606
- I love talking to you,

00:10:06.606 --> 00:10:08.374
because nobody else
has brought that up.

00:10:08.374 --> 00:10:10.710
Yes, okay.

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:15.281
This Biden team has been
the most tight-lipped,

00:10:15.281 --> 00:10:17.950
disciplined, no leaks,

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:19.151
no backbiting.

00:10:19.151 --> 00:10:20.720 align:start size:81% position:19%
I mean, people
are kind of bored.

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:21.654
Like, wait,

00:10:21.654 --> 00:10:22.722
we used to get, what happens,

00:10:22.722 --> 00:10:24.357 align:start size:88% position:13%
even in any
administration you would

00:10:24.357 --> 00:10:25.858
get a little bit of that.

00:10:25.858 --> 00:10:29.328
But on the campaign trail,
they were very disciplined.

00:10:29.328 --> 00:10:30.563
And I think that's
probably because

00:10:30.563 --> 00:10:33.466
they keep their
circles so small,

00:10:33.466 --> 00:10:36.035 align:start size:97% position:3%
but really pretty interesting
that even as it grew

00:10:36.035 --> 00:10:39.438
from the primary campaign
into the general election,

00:10:39.438 --> 00:10:43.209 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
still no leaks,
hardly any background,

00:10:43.209 --> 00:10:44.443 line:0%
you didn't get any color,

00:10:44.443 --> 00:10:46.479 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
like, what's going
on over there?

00:10:46.479 --> 00:10:48.247
About three weeks ago I said,

00:10:48.247 --> 00:10:50.916 align:start size:84% position:16%
you'll know that
there's some tension,

00:10:50.916 --> 00:10:53.819
or a little bit of elbow room,

00:10:53.819 --> 00:10:56.489 align:start size:84% position:16%
when you start seeing
background quotes

00:10:56.489 --> 00:10:59.458
from somebody close
to the White House,

00:10:59.458 --> 00:11:00.926
or within the White House,

00:11:00.926 --> 00:11:03.963
who says something
about Kamala Harris

00:11:03.963 --> 00:11:07.867
that would make it clear that
she is the Vice President.

00:11:07.867 --> 00:11:08.801
- [Dave] Mhm.

00:11:08.801 --> 00:11:10.069
- And so when I saw that,

00:11:10.069 --> 00:11:11.804
I'm like, ooh. To me,
that was kind of like,

00:11:11.804 --> 00:11:14.407
it was kind of the
first time you had

00:11:14.407 --> 00:11:16.976
seen a little bit of
a brush back pitch.

00:11:16.976 --> 00:11:18.577
And I don't know what's
going on internally,

00:11:18.577 --> 00:11:19.779
'cause you'd never see anybody.

00:11:19.779 --> 00:11:22.348
They don't talk, they
don't do anything.

00:11:22.348 --> 00:11:25.418 align:start size:97% position:3%
But that was definitely a
little bit of a just remember.

00:11:25.418 --> 00:11:28.888 align:start size:94% position:6%
And also if I were Kamala
Harris, I'd be mad.

00:11:30.322 --> 00:11:33.592 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then how it
happened is you have

00:11:33.592 --> 00:11:36.162 align:start size:88% position:13%
West wing versus
Vice-President's office.

00:11:36.162 --> 00:11:37.763 align:start size:84% position:16%
There's always a
little tension there.

00:11:37.763 --> 00:11:41.067
Even though they handled
it pretty well so far,

00:11:41.067 --> 00:11:42.835
and Joe Biden is actually quite,

00:11:43.903 --> 00:11:45.404
I'm gonna say solicitous,

00:11:45.404 --> 00:11:48.374 align:start size:97% position:3%
but he tries to be very
inclusive of Kamala Harris.

00:11:48.374 --> 00:11:51.610
So even though he is the
President, he will say,

00:11:52.845 --> 00:11:55.514
I did this, and he'll
stop himself and say,

00:11:55.514 --> 00:11:57.583
we did this.

00:11:57.583 --> 00:12:00.186 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think that's a
little bit out of respect,

00:12:01.387 --> 00:12:03.622
and also maybe just
his management style,

00:12:03.622 --> 00:12:06.292 align:start size:88% position:13%
that he wants it to look
like there's a team,

00:12:06.292 --> 00:12:07.259
and a shared effort.

00:12:07.259 --> 00:12:08.360
I think time will tell.

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:10.296
It's it's a little early to say.

00:12:10.296 --> 00:12:13.132 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So while you're giving
these guys credit on sort of

00:12:13.132 --> 00:12:15.401 align:start size:97% position:3%
the competency of how
they're managing the thing,

00:12:15.401 --> 00:12:19.805
how do you think we're doing
on the unity and healing front.

00:12:19.805 --> 00:12:23.342
That was the meme we got
starting on January 1st.

00:12:23.342 --> 00:12:24.977 align:start size:81% position:19%
Well, really I
guess on the 20th,

00:12:24.977 --> 00:12:26.479
but really it's starting
at the top of the year.

00:12:26.479 --> 00:12:27.313
It was like, okay,

00:12:27.313 --> 00:12:28.781
2020 is gone.

00:12:28.781 --> 00:12:30.716 align:start size:94% position:6%
Now we can begin the year
of unity and healing.

00:12:30.716 --> 00:12:31.951 align:start size:84% position:16%
That was the
messaging for awhile.

00:12:31.951 --> 00:12:33.219 align:start size:88% position:13%
We're not really hearing
about it anymore,

00:12:33.219 --> 00:12:36.922 align:start size:88% position:13%
but I never felt that
it really had any value,

00:12:36.922 --> 00:12:39.391
because we know if the shoe
had been on the other foot,

00:12:39.391 --> 00:12:42.828
this certainly wouldn't have
been about unity and healing.

00:12:42.828 --> 00:12:47.600
- And the bipartisan effort to
try to do a COVID relief plan

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:51.003 align:start size:97% position:3%
that they are now calling
the American rescue package.

00:12:51.003 --> 00:12:52.505 align:start size:81% position:19%
That was, like
the whole idea was

00:12:52.505 --> 00:12:54.006
that we're gonna
do this together.

00:12:54.006 --> 00:12:56.876 align:start size:94% position:6%
And pretty quickly it
was clear that Ron Klain,

00:12:56.876 --> 00:13:00.312 align:start size:97% position:3%
who was the Chief of Staff,
is not for that at all.

00:13:00.312 --> 00:13:04.049
Again, there's a little bit of
color from the meeting that,

00:13:04.049 --> 00:13:05.918 align:start size:94% position:6%
you remember when several
of those Republicans,

00:13:05.918 --> 00:13:07.353
Susan Collins, Romney,

00:13:07.353 --> 00:13:09.889
Portman, about seven of them.

00:13:09.889 --> 00:13:11.590
They'd go to the
Oval Office to have

00:13:11.590 --> 00:13:13.225
a meeting with Joe Biden to say,

00:13:13.225 --> 00:13:14.460
hey, look.

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:16.562
You say 1.9 trillion,
we say 600 billion.

00:13:16.562 --> 00:13:19.298
Let's talk, like maybe we
can figure out some things.

00:13:19.298 --> 00:13:21.333 align:start size:84% position:16%
And Susan Collins, the
Senator from Maine,

00:13:21.333 --> 00:13:23.402
reports that during
that entire meeting

00:13:23.402 --> 00:13:27.006 align:start size:84% position:16%
Ron Klain sat to the
President's side,

00:13:27.006 --> 00:13:29.008
out of eyesight from Biden,

00:13:30.176 --> 00:13:32.478
shaking his head the
whole time like nope.

00:13:32.478 --> 00:13:33.646
Nope.

00:13:33.646 --> 00:13:34.780 align:start size:84% position:16%
And she said she
knew in that meeting.

00:13:34.780 --> 00:13:36.215
She was like,

00:13:36.215 --> 00:13:39.118 align:start position:0%
they have no intention of
making this a bipartisan effort.

00:13:39.118 --> 00:13:40.419 align:start size:84% position:16%
Of course they
would love for some

00:13:40.419 --> 00:13:41.654
for some Republicans to say,

00:13:41.654 --> 00:13:42.488
oh yeah, sure.

00:13:42.488 --> 00:13:43.322
Okay, we'll do that.

00:13:45.524 --> 00:13:47.760
It's interesting, unity.

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:50.029
In any general election,

00:13:50.029 --> 00:13:52.932
you can find that a
majority of people,

00:13:52.932 --> 00:13:55.034
the vast majority
of people will say

00:13:55.034 --> 00:13:57.136
they want Congress
to work together,

00:13:57.136 --> 00:13:59.572
to get along, to do the
right thing together,

00:13:59.572 --> 00:14:00.940
and move the country forward.

00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:04.043
And then that lasts
for about three days.

00:14:04.043 --> 00:14:07.046 align:start size:97% position:3%
In this case, it didn't
even get there at the time.

00:14:07.046 --> 00:14:09.782 align:start size:94% position:6%
So it looks like we're
going to get $1.9 trillion

00:14:11.183 --> 00:14:15.287 align:start size:94% position:6%
of spending by a very slim
majority of Democrats.

00:14:15.287 --> 00:14:16.555
And I don't think
that you'll get

00:14:16.555 --> 00:14:18.090
I don't think you'll
get any Republicans

00:14:18.090 --> 00:14:19.391
to vote for it in the end.

00:14:20.826 --> 00:14:22.394
- So as someone that worked
in the government and had to,

00:14:22.394 --> 00:14:23.662 align:start size:84% position:16%
like, manage
messaging and stuff.

00:14:23.662 --> 00:14:24.997
When they come up
with these numbers,

00:14:24.997 --> 00:14:26.565
okay, we want 600 billion,

00:14:26.565 --> 00:14:28.667
we want 1.9 trillion,

00:14:28.667 --> 00:14:30.135 align:start size:72% position:28%
blah, blah,
blah.

00:14:30.135 --> 00:14:33.405 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's like, doesn't it
all kind of feel made up?

00:14:33.405 --> 00:14:36.141 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like where are these
numbers really coming from?

00:14:36.141 --> 00:14:38.510 align:start size:84% position:16%
Who's really paying
for it, like,

00:14:38.510 --> 00:14:40.045
that there's never a cost.

00:14:40.045 --> 00:14:41.447
There's never a
cost to be, like,

00:14:41.447 --> 00:14:43.249 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm the guy that wants
to cut some stuff,

00:14:43.249 --> 00:14:44.617
so you'd never do it,

00:14:44.617 --> 00:14:47.219 align:start size:88% position:13%
or there's never a
benefit in other words.

00:14:47.219 --> 00:14:48.387 align:start size:81% position:19%
Like, I'm going
to cut some stuff,

00:14:48.387 --> 00:14:49.655 align:start size:88% position:13%
no one's going to really
give you a credit.

00:14:49.655 --> 00:14:50.723
People just want more, and more,

00:14:50.723 --> 00:14:52.191
and more, and more.

00:14:52.191 --> 00:14:54.927
- Yeah, so you imagine
that Ron Klain would say,

00:14:54.927 --> 00:14:57.663
we can do $1.9 trillion,

00:14:57.663 --> 00:15:00.532
but don't you dare think
about going to $2 trillion,

00:15:00.532 --> 00:15:03.669 align:start size:84% position:16%
because $2 trillion
into the year is like,

00:15:03.669 --> 00:15:06.939 align:start size:84% position:16%
that's why things cost
$9.99 at the store.

00:15:06.939 --> 00:15:08.674
They don't cost $10,
'cause you'd be like,

00:15:08.674 --> 00:15:09.742
well, I'm not gonna pay $10.

00:15:09.742 --> 00:15:10.943
But $9.99?

00:15:10.943 --> 00:15:11.844
That's reasonable.

00:15:13.646 --> 00:15:15.748 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I think what
the Democrats think,

00:15:15.748 --> 00:15:17.883
the more you look at this bill,

00:15:17.883 --> 00:15:20.819
there's very little that's
specific for COVID relief,

00:15:20.819 --> 00:15:22.354 align:start size:84% position:16%
and they have a
particular philosophy,

00:15:22.354 --> 00:15:23.589
and they're sticking to it.

00:15:23.589 --> 00:15:24.790
They think that
the stimulus bill

00:15:24.790 --> 00:15:27.860
that Obama passed in
2009 was way too small,

00:15:27.860 --> 00:15:29.929 align:start size:97% position:3%
and that it did not do
enough to help the economy,

00:15:29.929 --> 00:15:32.665
and that was one of the
reasons that they say

00:15:32.665 --> 00:15:34.066
that the economy stalled.

00:15:34.066 --> 00:15:36.602 align:start size:84% position:16%
They don't talk
about the regulations

00:15:36.602 --> 00:15:37.937
that they put on businesses,

00:15:37.937 --> 00:15:40.105
all of the things that
they were trying to do

00:15:40.105 --> 00:15:43.242 align:start size:88% position:13%
to engineer the
economy as the problem.

00:15:43.242 --> 00:15:46.211
They think that not
passing more stimulus

00:15:46.211 --> 00:15:48.580
in 2009 was what hurt Obama.

00:15:48.580 --> 00:15:50.182
And Ron Klain came
into this saying

00:15:50.182 --> 00:15:52.251 align:start size:84% position:16%
that we are not going
to go too small,

00:15:52.251 --> 00:15:55.054 align:start size:81% position:19%
that the danger
of going too small

00:15:55.054 --> 00:15:57.923
is worse than the risk
of spending too much.

00:15:58.791 --> 00:15:59.992
That's our position.

00:15:59.992 --> 00:16:00.960 align:start size:84% position:16%
But if you start going
through this bill,

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:02.194
I just read this piece today

00:16:02.194 --> 00:16:04.663
by Isaac Shore in
National Review.

00:16:04.663 --> 00:16:05.898
There's a provision in there for

00:16:05.898 --> 00:16:10.903 align:start size:84% position:16%
federal employees
to be given $21,000,

00:16:11.503 --> 00:16:13.005
additional,

00:16:13.005 --> 00:16:15.874 align:start size:94% position:6%
if their children have had
to do school from home,

00:16:15.874 --> 00:16:18.477
and that's been a
hardship for them.

00:16:18.477 --> 00:16:20.212
It's outrageous.

00:16:20.212 --> 00:16:21.680
You check that out, I mean.

00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:23.749
- Swamp, I think that's
what we call the swamp.

00:16:23.749 --> 00:16:24.883
Is that what we call the swamp?

00:16:24.883 --> 00:16:26.218
- That is so much the swamp.

00:16:26.218 --> 00:16:27.286
Isaac Shore in National Review,

00:16:27.286 --> 00:16:28.520
check that one out.

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:29.722
There's the things,

00:16:29.722 --> 00:16:31.824
like the hundred
million dollars for

00:16:31.824 --> 00:16:35.260 align:start size:88% position:13%
the bridge that Chuck
Schumer wants in Canada.

00:16:35.260 --> 00:16:38.597
There's the hundred million
dollars that Nancy Pelosi

00:16:38.597 --> 00:16:40.632 align:start size:88% position:13%
wants for that
transportation project,

00:16:40.632 --> 00:16:43.202 align:start size:84% position:16%
from San Francisco
out to Silicon Valley,

00:16:43.202 --> 00:16:45.137
where I thought everybody
was working from home now.

00:16:45.137 --> 00:16:47.406
So why do we need that?

00:16:47.406 --> 00:16:49.174
- Actually, they've
all moved to Miami.

00:16:49.174 --> 00:16:50.642
That's the real truth.

00:16:50.642 --> 00:16:53.912
- Right, so that they
could save on taxes.

00:16:53.912 --> 00:16:54.747
- Yeah.

00:16:56.015 --> 00:16:58.017
- There is so much
in this bill that has

00:16:58.017 --> 00:17:00.052
nothing to do with COVID relief.

00:17:00.052 --> 00:17:04.623 align:start size:84% position:16%
And what, think back
to what happened,

00:17:04.623 --> 00:17:08.560
what was born out of the
stimulus package in 2009.

00:17:08.560 --> 00:17:09.361
The Tea Party.

00:17:10.696 --> 00:17:14.333
You know, they got the
grassroots attention.

00:17:14.333 --> 00:17:17.369
I have a feeling that this
could be the same thing.

00:17:17.369 --> 00:17:20.973
Now I also absolutely believe
that there are many people

00:17:20.973 --> 00:17:22.875
who have suffered
in this pandemic

00:17:22.875 --> 00:17:24.143
through no fault of their own.

00:17:24.143 --> 00:17:26.779
A lot of restaurant
owners, waitresses,

00:17:27.946 --> 00:17:30.149 align:start size:81% position:19%
people that worked
in hospitality,

00:17:30.149 --> 00:17:33.118 align:start size:88% position:13%
and we could target
specific money to them.

00:17:33.118 --> 00:17:35.888
But you go through this,
and it is a big wishlist.

00:17:35.888 --> 00:17:38.190
And part of the thing
is that Republicans

00:17:39.391 --> 00:17:40.692
disagreeing amongst each other,

00:17:40.692 --> 00:17:41.560
that's normal.

00:17:41.560 --> 00:17:42.728
It's going to happen,

00:17:42.728 --> 00:17:45.330 align:start size:84% position:16%
but that is like
catnip for reporters.

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:47.599
They're always going
to report on that.

00:17:47.599 --> 00:17:49.668 align:start size:88% position:13%
They don't do it as
much with the Democrats,

00:17:49.668 --> 00:17:52.971 align:start position:0%
though they have lots of
differences amongst themselves,

00:17:52.971 --> 00:17:56.108
but Republicans have
not really been able

00:17:56.108 --> 00:17:59.678
to figure out a way to drive
a message about this bill.

00:17:59.678 --> 00:18:01.580
And I don't know how you
feel about this, Dave,

00:18:01.580 --> 00:18:03.949 align:start size:84% position:16%
but the legislation
as written has

00:18:03.949 --> 00:18:07.453
a 73% approval rating
across the country.

00:18:08.587 --> 00:18:10.089
- Well, I think,

00:18:10.089 --> 00:18:13.125
to me, it's just that people
don't know what's in anything.

00:18:13.125 --> 00:18:14.860
It's sort of what you're
saying. And it's like, oh,

00:18:14.860 --> 00:18:16.562 align:start size:84% position:16%
you see a high number,
so you're like,

00:18:16.562 --> 00:18:19.264
oh, I kind of don't know
where the money comes from,

00:18:19.264 --> 00:18:21.467 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I guess it
doesn't come from me,

00:18:21.467 --> 00:18:23.635
and they'll just take
it from somewhere.

00:18:23.635 --> 00:18:26.071 align:start position:0%
And then if it's a high number,
that means I'll get more.

00:18:26.071 --> 00:18:28.640
So no politician ever
wants to be the one.

00:18:28.640 --> 00:18:30.008 align:start size:84% position:16%
Well, I guess there's
probably one guy

00:18:30.008 --> 00:18:31.343
that kind of does it
every now and again,

00:18:31.343 --> 00:18:33.779 align:start size:88% position:13%
which is Rand Paul is
the one guy that's like,

00:18:33.779 --> 00:18:36.348
oh, maybe we can't
pay for all of this,

00:18:36.348 --> 00:18:38.584
or maybe we shouldn't be
doing all of this stuff.

00:18:38.584 --> 00:18:40.519
And that's just sort
of how politics works.

00:18:40.519 --> 00:18:42.488 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And then you
sound mean if you--

00:18:42.488 --> 00:18:45.557 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Right, then you're
mean and cold.

00:18:45.557 --> 00:18:46.592
- That's right.

00:18:46.592 --> 00:18:47.526
- Then you hate people--

00:18:47.526 --> 00:18:48.760
- And we're about to see, like,

00:18:48.760 --> 00:18:50.462
right after this,

00:18:50.462 --> 00:18:53.432
they're going to pass a
infrastructure bill too.

00:18:54.500 --> 00:18:57.336 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it will be
probably $4 trillion.

00:18:58.470 --> 00:19:00.372 align:start size:88% position:13%
