00:27:45.497 --> 00:27:48.299
and then you can
look at it and say,

00:27:48.299 --> 00:27:49.534
oh yeah, that was,

00:27:49.534 --> 00:27:50.869
yeah, that was done. Oh
yeah, I did. Oh right.

00:27:50.869 --> 00:27:53.038
That wasn't very good.

00:27:53.038 --> 00:27:53.972
You got to feel it.

00:27:53.972 --> 00:27:55.940
And you have to see what it is.

00:27:55.940 --> 00:27:57.075
You have to be very
honest with yourself,

00:27:57.075 --> 00:27:59.110
because in today's day and age,

00:27:59.110 --> 00:28:01.046
the competition is fierce.

00:28:01.046 --> 00:28:02.947
You are going to face a lot of,

00:28:04.616 --> 00:28:06.084
you know, negativity,

00:28:06.084 --> 00:28:08.820
and people could bring
things up from your past.

00:28:08.820 --> 00:28:12.057
A lot of women will shy away.

00:28:12.057 --> 00:28:13.458
That's one of the
reasons that, like,

00:28:13.458 --> 00:28:15.694 align:start size:97% position:3%
Elise Stefanik, the
Congresswoman from New York,

00:28:15.694 --> 00:28:17.962 align:start size:88% position:13%
they focus specifically
on helping women

00:28:17.962 --> 00:28:20.498
who think they want
to run for office,

00:28:20.498 --> 00:28:22.367
but are concerned
about something

00:28:22.367 --> 00:28:24.903
coming up in their past and
hurting their current family.

00:28:24.903 --> 00:28:28.940 align:start position:0%
And they also are very
reluctant to ask to raise money.

00:28:28.940 --> 00:28:31.176 align:start position:0%
That's one of I think the
hardest things that people have.

00:28:31.176 --> 00:28:33.344
I think you might have a
hard time with that too.

00:28:33.344 --> 00:28:35.780
You got to call up your
friends, and job on them,

00:28:35.780 --> 00:28:37.882
and get them to give
you a million dollars

00:28:37.882 --> 00:28:39.684
for your campaign
that might not win.

00:28:39.684 --> 00:28:40.518
- [Dave] Yeah.

00:28:40.518 --> 00:28:41.386
- And so that's hard.

00:28:42.821 --> 00:28:45.590
But I do think that if people
are thinking about running,

00:28:45.590 --> 00:28:48.693 align:start size:84% position:16%
if you have a
loving relationship,

00:28:48.693 --> 00:28:52.130
a great family that is going
to stand by you no matter what,

00:28:52.130 --> 00:28:52.964
you can do it.

00:28:52.964 --> 00:28:53.998
You can get through it.

00:28:56.067 --> 00:28:59.738
- I think I'm going to stick
on YouTube for now, for now.

00:28:59.738 --> 00:29:01.439 align:start size:88% position:13%
Unless you're looking
to get back in the game,

00:29:01.439 --> 00:29:03.475 align:start size:97% position:3%
and if you want to be my
Chief of Staff or something,

00:29:03.475 --> 00:29:04.676
then we can go from there--

00:29:04.676 --> 00:29:05.643 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I'd be a very
good Chief of Staff.

00:29:05.643 --> 00:29:06.945
I would not be a good candidate.

00:29:06.945 --> 00:29:08.413 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'd be a great Chief
of Staff though.

00:29:08.413 --> 00:29:10.048
I'd be like Ron Klain
in the background,

00:29:10.048 --> 00:29:11.950
going nope, nope.

00:29:11.950 --> 00:29:13.451
- Yeah, Exactly.

00:29:13.451 --> 00:29:15.019 align:start size:94% position:6%
Exactly. So let's shift
to your book a little bit,

00:29:15.019 --> 00:29:17.622
because you were talking about
sort of your schedule change,

00:29:17.622 --> 00:29:19.357
and managing your life
and everything else.

00:29:19.357 --> 00:29:20.859 align:start size:94% position:6%
And when I was reading
through this this morning,

00:29:20.859 --> 00:29:23.695 align:start size:81% position:19%
and I've got it
right here, I had,

00:29:23.695 --> 00:29:26.931 align:start size:97% position:3%
there's a certain sort of
Jordan Peterson feel to this,

00:29:26.931 --> 00:29:29.501 align:start size:81% position:19%
about getting your
life in order.

00:29:29.501 --> 00:29:31.002
But, you know,

00:29:31.002 --> 00:29:33.772 align:start size:97% position:3%
you're obviously aiming this
more towards young women.

00:29:33.772 --> 00:29:36.374 align:start size:94% position:6%
Now you say, you say from
a former young woman.

00:29:36.374 --> 00:29:38.576
Am I allowed to ask a TV
host how old they are?

00:29:38.576 --> 00:29:39.611
Is that a question
that can be asked?

00:29:39.611 --> 00:29:41.146
- Sure, I know.

00:29:41.146 --> 00:29:43.181
So I love the subtitle, it
makes me laugh every time.

00:29:43.181 --> 00:29:47.285
And I also think it's true,
so I'll be 49 this year.

00:29:47.285 --> 00:29:48.520
So--

00:29:48.520 --> 00:29:50.288
- That's like, you're
still pretty young.

00:29:50.288 --> 00:29:51.222
- Kind of.

00:29:51.222 --> 00:29:52.991
But you think about people,

00:29:52.991 --> 00:29:56.127
like, what do you
learn from, say 22,

00:29:56.127 --> 00:29:58.196
when you start your first job,

00:29:58.196 --> 00:30:00.031
to 49 is a lot.

00:30:00.031 --> 00:30:01.733
You know, you go through a lot.

00:30:01.733 --> 00:30:05.370
And I get asked by a lot of
younger people for advice

00:30:05.370 --> 00:30:08.039
as they're trying to climb
their way up the ladder,

00:30:08.039 --> 00:30:10.508 align:start size:84% position:16%
or try to get their
sort of life together.

00:30:10.508 --> 00:30:12.243
And so I wrote the book,

00:30:12.243 --> 00:30:15.180 align:start size:97% position:3%
because all those young
women that come in my office,

00:30:15.180 --> 00:30:16.514
I could do it all day long.

00:30:16.514 --> 00:30:18.817 align:start size:81% position:19%
I could have a
mentoring business

00:30:18.817 --> 00:30:21.252
just sitting here taking calls,

00:30:21.252 --> 00:30:22.420
giving advice.

00:30:23.855 --> 00:30:26.691
But I just noticed that
they would worry so much,

00:30:26.691 --> 00:30:28.693
and they want a specific plan,

00:30:28.693 --> 00:30:30.795 align:start size:84% position:16%
which specific
plans do not exist.

00:30:32.130 --> 00:30:33.631
You can put yourself
in the right direction.

00:30:33.631 --> 00:30:36.201
So when I say in the title
everything will be okay?

00:30:37.202 --> 00:30:38.736
That is true,

00:30:38.736 --> 00:30:42.707 align:start size:97% position:3%
but you have to do some
things to make it that way.

00:30:42.707 --> 00:30:47.078 align:start size:97% position:3%
You have to make good personal
decisions, time management.

00:30:47.078 --> 00:30:50.381
I think that there's a lot
of freedom in discipline,

00:30:51.716 --> 00:30:53.651 align:start size:94% position:6%
and having some
principles and parameters

00:30:53.651 --> 00:30:54.786
in your life that can help you,

00:30:54.786 --> 00:30:56.487
but there's really ambitious,

00:30:56.487 --> 00:30:59.157
amazing young people out there.

00:30:59.157 --> 00:31:00.558 line:0%
And especially for young women.

00:31:00.558 --> 00:31:03.027 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
If you're born in
America, first of all,

00:31:03.027 --> 00:31:05.496 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
you already won
life's great lottery.

00:31:05.496 --> 00:31:06.631
- [Dave] Yeah, you're good.

00:31:06.631 --> 00:31:09.434 align:start size:88% position:13%
- If you are an
educated American woman,

00:31:09.434 --> 00:31:10.835
then all you got
to decide right now

00:31:10.835 --> 00:31:12.303
is how responsible
do you want to be,

00:31:12.303 --> 00:31:13.972
and how hard do
you want to work?

00:31:13.972 --> 00:31:15.506
Because you're in
the driver's seat.

00:31:15.506 --> 00:31:16.975
And I don't think a lot
of them realize that.

00:31:16.975 --> 00:31:18.676 align:start size:81% position:19%
You know, a lot
of them think that

00:31:20.111 --> 00:31:23.281
the guys are always going to
be promoted over the gals.

00:31:23.281 --> 00:31:24.616
That's not true.

00:31:24.616 --> 00:31:25.850
That the guys are going to get

00:31:25.850 --> 00:31:28.386
the promotions and the raises.

00:31:28.386 --> 00:31:30.088
Anymore, that's true.

00:31:30.088 --> 00:31:31.956
Many women are now the primary

00:31:31.956 --> 00:31:34.158
breadwinner of their families.

00:31:34.158 --> 00:31:37.595
And so we're in a little bit
of a cultural shift there,

00:31:37.595 --> 00:31:40.698
and it is time to step up.

00:31:40.698 --> 00:31:42.500
To be a leader, to be a manager,

00:31:42.500 --> 00:31:44.736
you got to show that you're
able and willing to do it,

00:31:44.736 --> 00:31:46.204
and resilient.

00:31:46.204 --> 00:31:48.873
The pandemic adds
another wrinkle.

00:31:48.873 --> 00:31:52.410 align:start size:81% position:19%
How do you network
in a pandemic?

00:31:52.410 --> 00:31:54.545
How do you get face
time with the boss?

00:31:54.545 --> 00:31:56.814
How do you figure out
what your next move is?

00:31:56.814 --> 00:31:59.584 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I talk about all sorts
of those things in the book.

00:31:59.584 --> 00:32:00.852
- Yeah.

00:32:00.852 --> 00:32:02.754
Do you think that the
pandemic did anything,

00:32:02.754 --> 00:32:04.022
broadly speaking,

00:32:04.022 --> 00:32:06.291
to women in a different
way than it did to men?

00:32:06.291 --> 00:32:09.928
So perhaps being home
more, homeschooling,

00:32:09.928 --> 00:32:11.162
whatever, you might be cooking,

00:32:11.162 --> 00:32:12.397
all those things, again,

00:32:12.397 --> 00:32:14.499
of course they're not
exclusive to women,

00:32:14.499 --> 00:32:16.567 align:start size:88% position:13%
but as you know, because
I toured with Jordan,

00:32:16.567 --> 00:32:20.038
I saw what so many young
men were dealing with.

00:32:20.038 --> 00:32:21.673
And now this is more about what

00:32:21.673 --> 00:32:23.141
young women are dealing with.

00:32:23.141 --> 00:32:25.243 align:start size:97% position:3%
Throw in a pandemic where
everybody's back in the house,

00:32:25.243 --> 00:32:26.678
and it's very different.

00:32:26.678 --> 00:32:27.912
- Yes.

00:32:27.912 --> 00:32:29.414
Some of the statistics
about how many women

00:32:29.414 --> 00:32:31.950
have left the workforce
will just blow your mind.

00:32:31.950 --> 00:32:33.618 align:start size:94% position:6%
That there are less women
in the workforce now

00:32:33.618 --> 00:32:35.520
than there were some
like 30 years ago.

00:32:35.520 --> 00:32:36.721
Now they'll come back.

00:32:36.721 --> 00:32:37.989
That will come back.

00:32:37.989 --> 00:32:39.891
But what do you lose
in that time frame?

00:32:39.891 --> 00:32:41.392
Right?

00:32:41.392 --> 00:32:43.328
Because new colleagues come,
new technology is introduced.

00:32:43.328 --> 00:32:44.562 align:start size:84% position:16%
We have a new
software system here.

00:32:44.562 --> 00:32:46.731
I don't even, I don't
even understand it.

00:32:46.731 --> 00:32:49.834 align:start size:88% position:13%
But you have to get
trained on those things.

00:32:49.834 --> 00:32:51.135
You come back to the workforce,

00:32:51.135 --> 00:32:53.071
and all of a sudden they
want to cut your salary

00:32:53.071 --> 00:32:56.574
by $10,000 because
you were at home.

00:32:56.574 --> 00:32:58.076
Now what's happened is,

00:32:58.076 --> 00:32:59.744
the reason a lot of women
had left the workforce

00:32:59.744 --> 00:33:02.113
is because kids
aren't in school.

00:33:02.113 --> 00:33:04.315
So if kids aren't in school,

00:33:04.315 --> 00:33:08.019
it usually will fall to
the woman, the mother,

00:33:08.019 --> 00:33:10.088
to be the one that
stays home to help

00:33:10.088 --> 00:33:12.824 align:start size:81% position:19%
with the work from
home schedule,

00:33:12.824 --> 00:33:15.393
sit on the Zoom, take
care of the family.

00:33:15.393 --> 00:33:17.362
And women have made
a lot of sacrifices.

00:33:17.362 --> 00:33:19.263
Men too, men too.

00:33:19.263 --> 00:33:21.165 align:start size:84% position:16%
But from the workplace
perspective, yes.

00:33:21.165 --> 00:33:22.867 align:start size:81% position:19%
If I could get one
piece of advice

00:33:24.202 --> 00:33:25.870
that I talk about in the book.

00:33:25.870 --> 00:33:27.939
A girlfriend of
mine was a global

00:33:27.939 --> 00:33:30.174 align:start size:81% position:19%
talent officer
for a big PR firm,

00:33:30.174 --> 00:33:32.377
and she has three boys.

00:33:32.377 --> 00:33:34.712
And she's one of those
friends that I look at

00:33:34.712 --> 00:33:37.081
as how in the world
does she do at all,

00:33:37.081 --> 00:33:38.916
one of those friends.

00:33:38.916 --> 00:33:41.319
So I asked her about
this issue with women.

00:33:41.319 --> 00:33:44.422 align:start size:97% position:3%
Now this is true
regardless of the pandemic.

00:33:44.422 --> 00:33:45.890 align:start size:84% position:16%
Let's say that
you're going to have

00:33:45.890 --> 00:33:47.658
to take a break from work.

00:33:47.658 --> 00:33:50.728 align:start size:88% position:13%
Whether it be because
of kids, work from home,

00:33:50.728 --> 00:33:54.332 align:start size:84% position:16%
or maybe you decide
that you want to spend

00:33:54.332 --> 00:33:57.535
the first few years
of your child's life

00:33:57.535 --> 00:33:59.537
at home and not
at the workplace.

00:34:00.738 --> 00:34:02.273
When you decide to go
back to the workplace,

00:34:02.273 --> 00:34:04.008 align:start size:81% position:19%
it's almost always
true that women

00:34:04.008 --> 00:34:07.545 align:start size:84% position:16%
will be looked at
as a difficult hire,

00:34:08.980 --> 00:34:10.882
because they might not be
available all the time.

00:34:10.882 --> 00:34:12.583
Work-share is a great idea,

00:34:12.583 --> 00:34:14.052 align:start size:94% position:6%
and some of the companies
are doing it well.

00:34:14.052 --> 00:34:15.953
The pandemic has
helped a little bit

00:34:15.953 --> 00:34:17.822
to make for some
more flexibility,

00:34:19.123 --> 00:34:21.559
but there will be a gap
in your resume, right?

00:34:21.559 --> 00:34:22.760
So it'll be like, I worked here,

00:34:22.760 --> 00:34:24.595 align:start size:84% position:16%
I worked here, but
then there's this gap.

00:34:24.595 --> 00:34:27.198
And now you're coming
back to the workplace.

00:34:27.198 --> 00:34:28.733
Oftentimes what Michelle told me

00:34:28.733 --> 00:34:31.736
is that they will be demoted,

00:34:31.736 --> 00:34:33.638 align:start size:84% position:16%
or cut in salary,
something like that.

00:34:33.638 --> 00:34:35.239 align:start size:84% position:16%
So she has this
great recommendation.

00:34:35.239 --> 00:34:36.707
I tell everybody about this,

00:34:36.707 --> 00:34:38.209 align:start size:84% position:16%
and it's true for
same-sex couples too.

00:34:38.209 --> 00:34:39.710
If you have a similar situation,

00:34:39.710 --> 00:34:41.179
if you are going
to take some time

00:34:41.179 --> 00:34:43.314
off from work to
help raise a baby.

00:34:44.515 --> 00:34:46.717 align:start size:88% position:13%
She says, if you're
going to take time off,

00:34:46.717 --> 00:34:49.587 align:start size:84% position:16%
start an LLC, little
small business,

00:34:49.587 --> 00:34:52.423 align:start size:84% position:16%
easy to set up, pretty
inexpensive really.

00:34:52.423 --> 00:34:53.858 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I don't know about
California prices

00:34:53.858 --> 00:34:56.894 align:start size:88% position:13%
but I think in New
Jersey, it's like $250.

00:34:56.894 --> 00:34:57.929
- I think Delaware.

00:34:57.929 --> 00:34:59.130
You want to do it in Delaware.

00:34:59.130 --> 00:35:00.398
- Good point.

00:35:00.398 --> 00:35:02.667
And she said have
a little business.

00:35:02.667 --> 00:35:03.768
So I would call mine, like,

00:35:03.768 --> 00:35:06.003
Dana Perino Communications.

00:35:06.003 --> 00:35:10.241
And you have a little
bit of opportunity

00:35:10.241 --> 00:35:11.976
then to take on a client or two,

00:35:11.976 --> 00:35:14.178
or a project here and there.

00:35:14.178 --> 00:35:17.148 align:start size:84% position:16%
But when you go back
to the workforce,

00:35:17.148 --> 00:35:20.651
you will not have a work gap.

00:35:20.651 --> 00:35:24.956 align:start position:0%
You will have an entrepreneurial
addition to the resume,

00:35:24.956 --> 00:35:27.592
so that the chances of
you being knocked back,

00:35:27.592 --> 00:35:30.795 align:start size:97% position:3%
either from a responsibility
standpoint or salary,

00:35:30.795 --> 00:35:32.263
is greatly decreased.

00:35:32.263 --> 00:35:34.198
And I thought that
was such good advice.

00:35:34.198 --> 00:35:36.400
I try to mention it always.

00:35:36.400 --> 00:35:38.302
- So how much what
you have in the book

00:35:38.302 --> 00:35:39.804
really is just sort of, like,

00:35:39.804 --> 00:35:41.873
think about your life clearly,

00:35:41.873 --> 00:35:44.675 align:start size:97% position:3%
because it seems to me
that a lot of young people,

00:35:44.675 --> 00:35:47.011
especially because of what's
happened in the last year,

00:35:47.011 --> 00:35:49.514 align:start size:88% position:13%
not only does the future
just look very murky,

00:35:49.514 --> 00:35:50.815
like, the future of the country,

00:35:50.815 --> 00:35:52.550
the future of their lives,

00:35:52.550 --> 00:35:53.751
like, a career,

00:35:53.751 --> 00:35:56.320
you know, any of
that kind of stuff.

00:35:56.320 --> 00:35:58.589
But also they don't know, like.

00:35:59.690 --> 00:36:01.726
what they want in a sense of,

00:36:01.726 --> 00:36:03.027
like, young women,

00:36:03.027 --> 00:36:05.463
maybe they've been
trained for too long

00:36:05.463 --> 00:36:09.534
to put too much emphasis on
career instead of family.

00:36:09.534 --> 00:36:11.035
Same could go for young men.

00:36:11.035 --> 00:36:13.437 align:start size:84% position:16%
That where they're
always being aimed at.

00:36:13.437 --> 00:36:16.040 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like, if you were a young
woman and you were just like,

00:36:16.040 --> 00:36:19.710
I just want to be the
best homemaker and
wife that I can be,

00:36:19.710 --> 00:36:21.312
and teach my kids,

00:36:21.312 --> 00:36:24.015 align:start size:81% position:19%
and cook, and keep
a great house.

00:36:24.015 --> 00:36:25.349
That's a very lofty thing.

00:36:25.349 --> 00:36:26.384
My mom raised three kids--

00:36:26.384 --> 00:36:27.585
- That's a job.

00:36:27.585 --> 00:36:28.886 align:start size:88% position:13%
- She did not work when
she was raising us,

00:36:28.886 --> 00:36:30.855
but then did go back
to work afterwards.

00:36:30.855 --> 00:36:32.089
She worked before, and then,

00:36:32.089 --> 00:36:33.691
but had like a 20
year gap on that

00:36:33.691 --> 00:36:35.326 align:start size:84% position:16%
while she was
raising three kids.

00:36:35.326 --> 00:36:37.028
To me, that was a
beautiful thing.

00:36:37.028 --> 00:36:39.797
But like, if you say
that as a woman now,

00:36:39.797 --> 00:36:42.867
it's kind of scoffed
at by the mainstream.

00:36:42.867 --> 00:36:46.737 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yes, though I think that's
changing a little bit.

00:36:46.737 --> 00:36:49.140
I do, especially as women
now have more choices,

00:36:49.140 --> 00:36:49.974
right?

00:36:49.974 --> 00:36:51.209
So, as I mentioned,

00:36:51.209 --> 00:36:52.543
a lot of women are now
the primary breadwinner.

00:36:52.543 --> 00:36:53.744
So that's actually,

00:36:53.744 --> 00:36:57.582 align:start size:88% position:13%
there's a cultural shift
happening there too.

00:36:57.582 --> 00:37:01.352 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you have a life partner
that is okay with that,

00:37:01.352 --> 00:37:02.887
you know, and supportive?

00:37:02.887 --> 00:37:06.357
And I think that that is
changing just a little bit.

00:37:06.357 --> 00:37:07.792
I have a piece of
advice in there

00:37:07.792 --> 00:37:09.860 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I call the
whiteboard incident.

00:37:09.860 --> 00:37:12.630 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it's because of something
that Peter did with me.

00:37:12.630 --> 00:37:15.967
When I was in San Diego,
when we lived in San Diego,

00:37:15.967 --> 00:37:18.536 align:start size:88% position:13%
I really wanted to work
for George W. Bush.

00:37:18.536 --> 00:37:20.071 align:start size:84% position:16%
I wanted to get back
to Washington DC,

00:37:20.071 --> 00:37:21.606
where I had worked before.

00:37:21.606 --> 00:37:22.707
Didn't quite know how to do it.

00:37:22.707 --> 00:37:24.642
I was working in PR.

00:37:24.642 --> 00:37:26.210
Really didn't enjoy it.

00:37:26.210 --> 00:37:28.346
And I couldn't really
see how to get ahead.

00:37:28.346 --> 00:37:30.381
And we lived in San Diego,

00:37:30.381 --> 00:37:32.049 align:start size:88% position:13%
which is where everybody
would love to live,

00:37:32.049 --> 00:37:33.284
except you're in LA,

00:37:33.284 --> 00:37:34.919
that's the other place
people want to live.

00:37:34.919 --> 00:37:37.388
- (laughs quickly) San Diego
sounds pretty sweet these days

00:37:37.388 --> 00:37:38.956
compared to what's
going on in LA--

00:37:38.956 --> 00:37:40.491
- Yeah, and the mayor,

00:37:40.491 --> 00:37:43.160
Kevin Faulkner, did a pretty
good job there during COVID,

00:37:43.160 --> 00:37:45.596 align:start position:0%
and he could be your competition
if you run for governor.

00:37:45.596 --> 00:37:47.298
- I know, I know.

00:37:47.298 --> 00:37:49.800
- So I went to DC, I
met with a few people,

00:37:49.800 --> 00:37:51.869
networked with folks that I used

00:37:51.869 --> 00:37:53.671
to work with on Capitol Hill.

00:37:53.671 --> 00:37:55.273 align:start size:84% position:16%
At that time, it
was August of ninth,

00:37:55.273 --> 00:37:57.808
I'm sorry, August of 2001.

00:37:57.808 --> 00:37:59.377 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then most of the
jobs were filled,

00:37:59.377 --> 00:38:00.911 align:start size:81% position:19%
but they'd keep
an eye out for me.

00:38:00.911 --> 00:38:03.714
And I come home and think I
just really want to do this.

00:38:03.714 --> 00:38:06.317 align:start size:84% position:16%
So Peter says, let's
do an experiment.

00:38:06.317 --> 00:38:10.588
He gets the whiteboard out with
this little erasable marker.

00:38:10.588 --> 00:38:13.591
And he says, tell me all the
things you want to do in a job.

00:38:13.591 --> 00:38:16.861
So I listed the things
I like to do in my work.

00:38:16.861 --> 00:38:19.196
Tell me all the things you
don't want to do in a job.

00:38:19.196 --> 00:38:20.765
So I listed all of those things.

00:38:20.765 --> 00:38:24.335
And then we assigned a
value to those things.

00:38:24.335 --> 00:38:26.704
And then it became very
clear, you add it up.

00:38:27.838 --> 00:38:30.608 align:start size:84% position:16%
Staying in San Diego
was like a zero.

00:38:31.776 --> 00:38:34.478
Doing anything in
DC was like a nine.

00:38:35.546 --> 00:38:36.480
I'm like, oh,

00:38:36.480 --> 00:38:38.082
when you look at it that way.

00:38:38.082 --> 00:38:39.350
Interesting.

00:38:39.350 --> 00:38:41.218
I also mentored a young
woman not too long ago,

00:38:41.218 --> 00:38:42.353
and she was sort of following

00:38:42.353 --> 00:38:44.789
in the footsteps of her family,

00:38:44.789 --> 00:38:46.190
of medical professionals.

00:38:47.391 --> 00:38:51.462
And at the end of
the chat, I said,

00:38:51.462 --> 00:38:54.131
if you weren't going to do
this, what would you do?

00:38:55.366 --> 00:38:56.200
She goes, oh,

00:38:56.200 --> 00:38:59.070
I am so into psychology,

00:38:59.070 --> 00:39:02.206
and I bought these books, and
I've read all these things,

00:39:02.206 --> 00:39:03.941 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I've been listening
to the podcasts,

00:39:03.941 --> 00:39:05.710
and I was thinking about
getting a Master's degree,

00:39:05.710 --> 00:39:07.611 align:start size:84% position:16%
because I've just
so interested in it.

00:39:07.611 --> 00:39:09.146
I said, well wait,

00:39:09.146 --> 00:39:10.815
Maybe you should be doing that.

00:39:10.815 --> 00:39:11.882
(laughing)

00:39:11.882 --> 00:39:12.717
Right?

00:39:12.717 --> 00:39:14.185
- [Dave] Right, right.

00:39:14.185 --> 00:39:17.121
- And so there's just so
much freedom now for people

00:39:17.121 --> 00:39:19.924 align:start size:84% position:16%
to be able to do so
many different things.

00:39:19.924 --> 00:39:21.592
One last story I'll mention.

00:39:21.592 --> 00:39:24.962 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's a young woman
that I know who graduated

00:39:24.962 --> 00:39:28.499
in December of 2019
in event management.

00:39:28.499 --> 00:39:29.700
Okay.

00:39:29.700 --> 00:39:31.102 align:start size:84% position:16%
She gets a job, she's
here in New York,

00:39:31.102 --> 00:39:32.737
she's going to do events.

00:39:32.737 --> 00:39:34.438 align:start size:84% position:16%
And she was working
for a company

00:39:34.438 --> 00:39:35.740 align:start size:81% position:19%
that did events
all over the city,

00:39:35.740 --> 00:39:39.510
big events, like
runway type things,

00:39:39.510 --> 00:39:41.846
fashion week, things like that.

00:39:41.846 --> 00:39:43.114
And what happens in February of,

00:39:43.114 --> 00:39:47.051
the end of February
2020, no events.

00:39:48.853 --> 00:39:51.455
I think that we are
now on our one year

00:39:51.455 --> 00:39:53.524
anniversary of our
two week lockdown.

00:39:53.524 --> 00:39:54.358 line:0%
(laughs quietly)

00:39:54.358 --> 00:39:55.192 line:0%
- [Dave] Yep.

00:39:55.192 --> 00:39:57.161 line:0%
- So she lost her job.

00:39:58.295 --> 00:40:01.031 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
She also ended up
with a skiing injury,

00:40:02.733 --> 00:40:05.403 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
so she went home to
live with her parents.

00:40:05.403 --> 00:40:07.772 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
And she could have
been quite despondent,

00:40:09.140 --> 00:40:11.609 align:start size:94% position:6%
but she was watching
these nurses and doctors,

00:40:11.609 --> 00:40:14.078 align:start size:88% position:13%
and she knew that in
her area, in New Jersey,

00:40:14.078 --> 00:40:15.579
they needed more masks.

00:40:15.579 --> 00:40:17.014
Her mother or grandmother was an

00:40:17.014 --> 00:40:18.916
interior designer
who had retired,

00:40:18.916 --> 00:40:20.618 align:start size:81% position:19%
and she had all
this extra fabric.

00:40:20.618 --> 00:40:22.720
So she had a sewing machine
and she's kind of hands on,

00:40:22.720 --> 00:40:24.388 align:start size:97% position:3%
so while she was recovering
from her surgery,

00:40:24.388 --> 00:40:26.090 align:start size:84% position:16%
she just made
masks all day long.

00:40:26.090 --> 00:40:28.325 align:start size:84% position:16%
Making masks,
donating them around.

00:40:28.325 --> 00:40:29.794
But they were super cute.

00:40:29.794 --> 00:40:31.595 align:start size:97% position:3%
So then people were like,
where can I get one of those?

00:40:31.595 --> 00:40:32.797
Where can I?

00:40:32.797 --> 00:40:34.298 align:start size:81% position:19%
So she started
selling the masks,

00:40:34.298 --> 00:40:37.501
and then she ended up with so
much business that she hired

00:40:37.501 --> 00:40:39.303
a couple of other young
women to work for her,

00:40:39.303 --> 00:40:42.173 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then she bought
an embroidery machine,

00:40:42.173 --> 00:40:43.741
and now she's been doing that.

00:40:43.741 --> 00:40:47.211
And who knows if she'll ever
go back to event management.

00:40:47.211 --> 00:40:48.612
You know, she found
a whole new thing,

00:40:48.612 --> 00:40:52.183 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I love the resilience
and the spirit.

00:40:52.183 --> 00:40:56.187 align:start position:0%
And also she was unconditionally
loved by her family.

00:40:57.621 --> 00:41:01.225
And it's okay to rely on your
family in times like this.

00:41:02.293 --> 00:41:03.761
- Yeah.

00:41:03.761 --> 00:41:04.929
And that's one of the things
that's been so hard for people,

00:41:04.929 --> 00:41:07.131 align:start size:84% position:16%
because in many
cases we're seriously

00:41:07.131 --> 00:41:09.567
separated from our family.

00:41:09.567 --> 00:41:13.037
I actually haven't seen my
parents since this began,

00:41:13.037 --> 00:41:15.706
because they finally
just got the vaccines,

00:41:15.706 --> 00:41:16.674
and, you know,

00:41:16.674 --> 00:41:17.942
they're in their early 70's,

00:41:17.942 --> 00:41:18.609
so it just wasn't
going to happen--

00:41:18.609 --> 00:41:20.110
- Me too.

00:41:20.110 --> 00:41:20.978 align:start size:94% position:6%
- I'm finally gonna see
them in a couple of weeks.

00:41:20.978 --> 00:41:21.912 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Good.
- But just lose,

00:41:21.912 --> 00:41:23.113
I think losing that connection,

00:41:23.113 --> 00:41:24.515
'cause, you know,

00:41:24.515 --> 00:41:26.617 align:start size:97% position:3%
people think it can be
replaced by FaceTime and Zoom,

00:41:26.617 --> 00:41:28.118
and it just can't.

00:41:28.118 --> 00:41:29.887 align:start size:97% position:3%
- No. And the other thing
that's happened for a lot of

00:41:29.887 --> 00:41:31.655 align:start size:88% position:13%
young people when you're
on zoom all the time

00:41:31.655 --> 00:41:35.392 align:start size:97% position:3%
is there's a lot of anxiety
about your appearance.

00:41:36.527 --> 00:41:38.295 align:start size:84% position:16%
Obviously I'm in
full hair and makeup

00:41:38.295 --> 00:41:40.364 align:start size:84% position:16%
from the show I did
earlier from Fox News,

00:41:40.364 --> 00:41:43.801 align:start size:84% position:16%
and so this is not how
I would normally be

00:41:43.801 --> 00:41:45.703
if I was working from my office,

00:41:45.703 --> 00:41:47.404
and I wasn't on TV.

00:41:49.106 --> 00:41:52.810
The anxiety about performing

00:41:52.810 --> 00:41:54.345 align:start size:84% position:16%
and being on camera
all the time.

00:41:54.345 --> 00:41:58.816
You think of a lot of people
are kind of introverts, right?

00:41:58.816 --> 00:42:01.519
Zoom gets them way out
of their comfort zone.

00:42:01.519 --> 00:42:02.753
If you have to be on a Zoom

00:42:02.753 --> 00:42:04.855
for three hours a
day with your team,

00:42:04.855 --> 00:42:08.392
it's been hard on
a lot of people.

00:42:08.392 --> 00:42:11.362 align:start size:94% position:6%
And also presenting in
front of that many people,

00:42:11.362 --> 00:42:13.430
instead of just doing it
in front of your team,

00:42:13.430 --> 00:42:16.734 align:start size:97% position:3%
or your board, in the
meeting and conference room.

00:42:16.734 --> 00:42:18.469 align:start size:84% position:16%
Now you're doing it
in front of everybody.

00:42:18.469 --> 00:42:20.337 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that anxiety
about public speaking

00:42:20.337 --> 00:42:22.172
is something that
I also talk about,

00:42:22.172 --> 00:42:24.441
especially because one
thing that's different

00:42:24.441 --> 00:42:27.845 align:start position:0%
from anything that you and
Jordan would have talked about.

00:42:27.845 --> 00:42:29.079
One of the things,

00:42:29.079 --> 00:42:31.549
one of the pieces of
advice I give is you,

00:42:31.549 --> 00:42:33.984
women have to find
their strong voice.

00:42:35.085 --> 00:42:37.688 align:start size:84% position:16%
So we have a naturally
higher register.

00:42:40.157 --> 00:42:42.826
For some, especially on a Zoom,

00:42:42.826 --> 00:42:45.429
or on camera,

00:42:45.429 --> 00:42:47.464
the voice can be grating.

00:42:49.033 --> 00:42:51.402 align:start size:81% position:19%
And a lot of young
women get into

00:42:51.402 --> 00:42:53.671
this habit early on
from their teens,

00:42:53.671 --> 00:42:55.673
and it's kind of following
them into the workplace,

00:42:55.673 --> 00:42:57.875
with, I call it up speak.

00:42:57.875 --> 00:42:59.076
Talk like this 'cause they're

00:42:59.076 --> 00:43:01.512 align:start size:84% position:16%
not really sure
about all the things.

00:43:01.512 --> 00:43:03.213
We used to call it
valley girl talk.

00:43:03.213 --> 00:43:04.048
- Yeah.

00:43:05.482 --> 00:43:08.185 align:start size:94% position:6%
- I'm just telling you,
you will not get promoted,

00:43:08.185 --> 00:43:11.589 align:start size:84% position:16%
or chosen to go
meet the new client,

00:43:11.589 --> 00:43:14.758
or go on the big business
trip if you talk like that.

00:43:14.758 --> 00:43:16.560
And a lot of people, Dave,

00:43:16.560 --> 00:43:18.829
they don't even realize
that they're doing it.

00:43:18.829 --> 00:43:21.365 align:start size:84% position:16%
And if you can gently
point out to them

00:43:22.366 --> 00:43:24.702
that they're speaking this way,

00:43:24.702 --> 00:43:26.103 align:start size:84% position:16%
they'll turn it
around immediately.

00:43:26.103 --> 00:43:26.904
They can turn it off.

00:43:26.904 --> 00:43:28.105
It's like, well,

00:43:28.105 --> 00:43:29.406 align:start size:84% position:16%
I do that? I didn't
know I do that.

00:43:29.406 --> 00:43:31.008 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then when they get
with their friends,

00:43:31.008 --> 00:43:33.077
they could fall back into it.

00:43:33.077 --> 00:43:35.312 align:start size:84% position:16%
But I'm really,
really big on this,

00:43:35.312 --> 00:43:36.580
because I see it.

00:43:36.580 --> 00:43:37.414
I'm like, oh,

00:43:38.582 --> 00:43:40.284
if she doesn't stop
that, she's never,

00:43:40.284 --> 00:43:42.219
never going to get promoted.

00:43:42.219 --> 00:43:43.487
- But that's like,

00:43:43.487 --> 00:43:44.755
that's like one of the
ones that it's kind of

00:43:44.755 --> 00:43:46.624 align:start size:88% position:13%
hard to say to somebody
these days, right?

00:43:46.624 --> 00:43:50.327 align:start size:97% position:3%
'Cause it feels like an
affront to their very being,

00:43:50.327 --> 00:43:52.830
the way you sound,
the way you speak.

00:43:52.830 --> 00:43:54.798 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah, maybe there
will be an HR violation.

00:43:54.798 --> 00:43:55.633
(laughs quietly)

00:43:55.633 --> 00:43:56.500
- Yeah.

00:43:56.500 --> 00:43:57.468
No, that's what I mean.

00:43:57.468 --> 00:43:58.435
Like they're going to, yeah,

00:43:58.435 --> 00:44:00.204
This is my natural affect.

00:44:00.204 --> 00:44:03.173 align:start size:94% position:6%
- But I've taken a couple
of young women aside,

00:44:03.173 --> 00:44:06.377
and you can make it
kind of light, sweet.

00:44:07.678 --> 00:44:10.481
I feel like we have an
obligation to help them.

00:44:10.481 --> 00:44:12.916 align:start size:84% position:16%
If you have someone
in your life,

00:44:12.916 --> 00:44:14.752
if it's your daughter,
or your granddaughter,

00:44:14.752 --> 00:44:16.820 align:start size:94% position:6%
or your niece, or somebody
that works for you.

00:44:16.820 --> 00:44:18.022
Maybe don't even tell them,

00:44:18.022 --> 00:44:19.690 align:start size:84% position:16%
just get them the
book (laughs quickly),

00:44:19.690 --> 00:44:21.225
and let them see.

00:44:21.225 --> 00:44:23.994 align:start size:84% position:16%
Hopefully they'll come
to it on their own.

00:44:23.994 --> 00:44:25.929 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I'm telling you, I
know that this has worked.

00:44:25.929 --> 00:44:27.464
There's a young woman
who used to work for me

00:44:27.464 --> 00:44:29.199
who definitely used to do that.

00:44:30.401 --> 00:44:32.903
I also think it's a sign
of a lack of confidence,

00:44:32.903 --> 00:44:36.640
because if you say something
in the form of a question,

00:44:36.640 --> 00:44:38.809
then you don't have
to own the opinion.

00:44:38.809 --> 00:44:40.210
Do you see what I mean?

00:44:40.210 --> 00:44:41.178
Like, I don't know.

00:44:41.178 --> 00:44:43.514
I'm just asking questions.

00:44:43.514 --> 00:44:46.450
If you say it that way,
you don't have to own it.

00:44:46.450 --> 00:44:47.918
- Yeah.

00:44:47.918 --> 00:44:49.153 align:start size:97% position:3%
I thought it was a really
interesting part of the book,

00:44:49.153 --> 00:44:50.487
because it reminded
me of sort of why

00:44:50.487 --> 00:44:53.524
I can't stand listening to NPR,

00:44:53.524 --> 00:44:56.160
because they do that thing too,

00:44:56.160 --> 00:44:59.029
and everything sort of
sounds like a question,

00:44:59.029 --> 00:45:02.866 align:start size:88% position:13%
and like it's never a
definitive proclamation,

00:45:02.866 --> 00:45:05.536
and I can't stand it.

00:45:05.536 --> 00:45:07.004
- Yeah.

00:45:07.004 --> 00:45:09.840
I mean, actually I don't listen
to NPR for a lot of reasons.

00:45:09.840 --> 00:45:11.442
(laughs quickly) Now
that you say that--

00:45:11.442 --> 00:45:13.277 align:start size:84% position:16%
- That's one of
many, many reasons.

00:45:13.277 --> 00:45:14.778
Yeah.

00:45:14.778 --> 00:45:16.113
- No, but now that you say
that, morning edition, Yeah.

00:45:16.113 --> 00:45:17.648
On morning edition then.

00:45:17.648 --> 00:45:19.116 align:start size:88% position:13%
Yeah, and even when
they ask the question--

00:45:19.116 --> 00:45:20.584
- That tone.

00:45:20.584 --> 00:45:21.885 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, even when they
asked the question

00:45:21.885 --> 00:45:23.020 align:start size:81% position:19%
and they know what
the answer is,

00:45:23.020 --> 00:45:23.854
they still do it.

00:45:23.854 --> 00:45:25.022
You know what I mean?

00:45:25.022 --> 00:45:26.223
Like, it's all scripted.

00:45:27.891 --> 00:45:29.259
- It's the worst
kind of scripted.

00:45:29.259 --> 00:45:32.396 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I was really interested
in chapter four,

00:45:32.396 --> 00:45:34.531 align:start size:84% position:16%
because it's how do
you find a role model?

00:45:34.531 --> 00:45:35.699
And as you may remember,

00:45:35.699 --> 00:45:38.402 align:start size:88% position:13%
chapter nine of my
book was find a mentor.

00:45:38.402 --> 00:45:39.603
- Yep, yep.

00:45:39.603 --> 00:45:40.838 align:start size:81% position:19%
- And that was
not a chapter that

00:45:40.838 --> 00:45:42.206
I had intended to
put in my book,

00:45:42.206 --> 00:45:43.674
but what happened
with me and Jordan

00:45:43.674 --> 00:45:47.244
was sort of an accidental
mentorship, I suppose,

00:45:47.244 --> 00:45:49.246
because we happened to
be on tour together,

00:45:49.246 --> 00:45:50.781
and it just kind of happened.

00:45:50.781 --> 00:45:52.816
But that you made a point of
saying, how do you find it,

00:45:52.816 --> 00:45:56.053
like this is something
that you've got to do.

00:45:56.053 --> 00:46:00.057
Do you think everybody needs
a mentor, needs a role model?

00:46:00.057 --> 00:46:01.391
- Yes I do.

00:46:01.391 --> 00:46:03.193 align:start size:84% position:16%
But I don't think
that the relationships

00:46:03.193 --> 00:46:06.530
have to be as formal as
some people might think.

00:46:06.530 --> 00:46:08.132
A lot of companies now will have

00:46:08.132 --> 00:46:10.167
these formal mentoring programs,

00:46:10.167 --> 00:46:11.635
and that's great.

00:46:11.635 --> 00:46:12.870
Although sometimes I don't
think they're really working.

00:46:12.870 --> 00:46:16.273 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like, I know of this
one program where

00:46:16.273 --> 00:46:18.909
there's somebody that
works in production,

00:46:18.909 --> 00:46:20.110
and they've been paired with

00:46:20.110 --> 00:46:21.411 align:start size:81% position:19%
somebody who works
in accounting,

00:46:22.780 --> 00:46:25.516 align:start size:94% position:6%
but the person, that
just doesn't really work.

00:46:25.516 --> 00:46:27.384 align:start size:94% position:6%
Now it's good to meet
people around the office,

00:46:27.384 --> 00:46:29.386
and I'm big on networking,
and building your network,

00:46:29.386 --> 00:46:31.121
and meet as many
people as you can.

00:46:32.156 --> 00:46:33.390
But I, yes,

00:46:33.390 --> 00:46:34.691
I absolutely think that
you need role models.

00:46:34.691 --> 00:46:36.960
I think back to my childhood.

00:46:36.960 --> 00:46:39.229
I really wanted to be an
anchor, a news anchor.

00:46:39.229 --> 00:46:40.898
I love Diane Sawyer.

00:46:42.032 --> 00:46:43.767
She had worked in the
Nixon White House.

00:46:43.767 --> 00:46:45.335 align:start size:84% position:16%
A lot of people
don't remember that.

00:46:45.335 --> 00:46:46.804
I liked how she carried herself,

00:46:46.804 --> 00:46:49.506 align:start size:81% position:19%
her story telling,
her journalism.

00:46:49.506 --> 00:46:51.875
I thought she had such
gravitas and dignity.

00:46:51.875 --> 00:46:55.479 align:start size:97% position:3%
I also even thought of, this
is a fictional character,

00:46:55.479 --> 00:46:58.615 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I love Cybill
Shepherd in Moonlighting.

00:46:58.615 --> 00:46:59.783
- Oh yeah!

00:46:59.783 --> 00:47:00.584
Bruce Willis.

00:47:00.584 --> 00:47:01.385
Yeah, yeah--

00:47:01.385 --> 00:47:02.619
- She was the boss lady.

00:47:02.619 --> 00:47:04.655
And I was sort of
like, you know,

00:47:04.655 --> 00:47:08.158 align:start size:94% position:6%
there's a saying in
women's mentoring called,

00:47:08.158 --> 00:47:12.529
or that says you have
to see her to be her.

00:47:13.964 --> 00:47:14.998
That's another reason, you know,

00:47:14.998 --> 00:47:16.333
I like to do the mentoring.

00:47:17.501 --> 00:47:20.571 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I'm constantly
finding role models.

00:47:20.571 --> 00:47:22.372 align:start size:88% position:13%
I have one now that I
write about in the book,

00:47:22.372 --> 00:47:23.974
her name is Ann Gloag.

00:47:23.974 --> 00:47:26.443
She's now Dame Ann
Gloag, I should say.

00:47:26.443 --> 00:47:28.445 align:start size:88% position:13%
I should say a
self-made business woman

00:47:28.445 --> 00:47:31.148
in Scotland who has
the biggest heart,

00:47:31.148 --> 00:47:33.517
and as her company, Stagecoach,

00:47:33.517 --> 00:47:34.785
the busing company,

00:47:34.785 --> 00:47:39.122 align:start size:97% position:3%
as it's grown
internationally, she got the,

00:47:39.122 --> 00:47:43.460
her brothers and her split
it up, the responsibilities.

00:47:43.460 --> 00:47:44.761
So they expanded into Europe.

00:47:44.761 --> 00:47:46.730
She did Europe and Africa,

00:47:46.730 --> 00:47:48.932
and her brothers did
Asia and South America.

00:47:50.100 --> 00:47:51.168
So she went to Africa.

00:47:51.168 --> 00:47:52.870
She had been a nurse in a,

00:47:52.870 --> 00:47:56.807 align:start size:88% position:13%
they call it in England
a burns unit for,

00:47:56.807 --> 00:47:57.975
I don't know what we
would call it here.

00:47:57.975 --> 00:47:59.443 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't think we
have a specific name.

00:47:59.443 --> 00:48:03.547 align:start position:0%
But she treated people that
had wounds from burns and fire.

00:48:03.547 --> 00:48:05.449
She goes to Africa.

00:48:05.449 --> 00:48:08.819 align:start size:84% position:16%
First of all, she
refuses to pay bribes.

00:48:08.819 --> 00:48:10.621
Will not do it.

00:48:10.621 --> 00:48:15.626 align:start size:97% position:3%
And this has never happened
before in the busing contracts

00:48:16.793 --> 00:48:18.562
that are happening in
Kenya and Tanzania,

00:48:18.562 --> 00:48:20.297
and they said, well,

00:48:20.297 --> 00:48:24.001
then your buses are not going
to be released from the port.

00:48:24.001 --> 00:48:25.569
And she says, fine,

00:48:25.569 --> 00:48:28.238
and we'll make sure
that you're blamed.

00:48:28.238 --> 00:48:29.640
She is tiny.

00:48:29.640 --> 00:48:30.807
I'm short, I'm five,

00:48:30.807 --> 00:48:32.876 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think just a little
smaller than me,

00:48:32.876 --> 00:48:34.244
really, really small person.

00:48:34.244 --> 00:48:35.712
She's got such guts.

00:48:35.712 --> 00:48:38.348 align:start size:97% position:3%
But what I really also love
about her is her heart.

00:48:38.348 --> 00:48:42.185 align:start size:94% position:6%
She saw what was happening
for early childhood

00:48:42.185 --> 00:48:44.354 align:start size:88% position:13%
development and maternal
health in Africa,

00:48:44.354 --> 00:48:47.324
and she was just determined
to do something about it.

00:48:47.324 --> 00:48:50.427
So not only is she just a
terrific business woman,

00:48:50.427 --> 00:48:52.562 align:start size:84% position:16%
I feel like she
has such a gracious

00:48:52.562 --> 00:48:54.431
way of handling her time,

00:48:54.431 --> 00:48:55.666
her work-life balance,

00:48:55.666 --> 00:49:00.437
where she gives a ton
with accountability.

00:49:00.437 --> 00:49:02.139 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, nobody
gets the free money,

00:49:02.139 --> 00:49:05.609
but she has a few clinics and
an orphanage that she runs.

00:49:05.609 --> 00:49:07.778 align:start size:84% position:16%
She runs the
business, and she has,

00:49:07.778 --> 00:49:10.180
I think, six children,

00:49:10.180 --> 00:49:12.616 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then several that
she's adopted from Africa.

00:49:12.616 --> 00:49:15.152
And I see her as sort of a,

00:49:16.553 --> 00:49:18.155
not a peer role model,

00:49:18.155 --> 00:49:19.656
but, you know,

00:49:19.656 --> 00:49:22.592 line:0%
even somebody that's
a former younger woman

00:49:23.694 --> 00:49:25.329 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
can have role
models to this day.

00:49:25.329 --> 00:49:27.831 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
'Cause I always want
to be improving.

00:49:27.831 --> 00:49:29.333 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's why I listened
to, you know,

00:49:29.333 --> 00:49:31.568
I watch your show, I
listen to podcasts,

00:49:31.568 --> 00:49:34.371
I am constantly reading.

00:49:34.371 --> 00:49:37.107
I don't ever want
to stop learning.

00:49:37.107 --> 00:49:39.843
And meeting her and
getting to know her

00:49:39.843 --> 00:49:42.412
has really enriched my life.

00:49:42.412 --> 00:49:43.647
- Wait, Dana,

00:49:43.647 --> 00:49:45.182
are you telling me you
don't know it all at 49?

00:49:45.182 --> 00:49:46.516 align:start size:84% position:16%
You haven't perfected
the whole thing?

00:49:46.516 --> 00:49:47.517
(laughs quickly)

00:49:47.517 --> 00:49:48.785
You haven't done it yet?

00:49:48.785 --> 00:49:50.420
- I don't know if
you're like this.

00:49:50.420 --> 00:49:52.189
I feel like I wake up every day

00:49:52.189 --> 00:49:54.725
knowing less than I
knew the day before.

00:49:54.725 --> 00:49:56.760 align:start size:84% position:16%
I feel like the
universe of knowledge

00:49:56.760 --> 00:49:59.329
is just growing
so exponentially.

00:49:59.329 --> 00:50:01.064
I feel I have so much to learn.

00:50:01.064 --> 00:50:02.799
I never feel like
I have read enough.

00:50:02.799 --> 00:50:05.736
And usually I have read more
than anybody else in the room,

00:50:05.736 --> 00:50:08.805
but I always feel like
I'm a little bit behind.

00:50:09.973 --> 00:50:11.842
- I asked my friend
who, as you know,

00:50:11.842 --> 00:50:13.310
recently passed, Larry King.

00:50:13.310 --> 00:50:14.678 align:start size:81% position:19%
Many times I asked
him, you know,

00:50:14.678 --> 00:50:16.480
what did he think the
meaning of life was?

00:50:16.480 --> 00:50:17.748 align:start size:81% position:19%
And he always said
the same thing.

00:50:17.748 --> 00:50:20.183
The only thing I know
is that I do not know.

00:50:20.183 --> 00:50:21.718
- I do not know.

00:50:21.718 --> 00:50:25.622
I just read a book by Adam
Grant called Think Again,

00:50:25.622 --> 00:50:28.658
The Power of Knowing
What You Don't Know.

00:50:28.658 --> 00:50:30.761 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Mhm.
- That was great.

00:50:30.761 --> 00:50:32.229
And it kind of takes
some pressure off.

00:50:32.229 --> 00:50:33.163
You can say said, like,

00:50:33.163 --> 00:50:33.964
I don't know about, I'm curious.

00:50:33.964 --> 00:50:35.198
Let me learn.

00:50:35.198 --> 00:50:36.533
Talk to me, tell
me what you know.

00:50:36.533 --> 00:50:37.501
How do you see it?

00:50:37.501 --> 00:50:38.935
How do you feel about it?

00:50:38.935 --> 00:50:40.804 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that kind of approach
I think can help,

00:50:40.804 --> 00:50:43.407 align:start size:97% position:3%
going back to what we started
with in terms of unity.

00:50:44.608 --> 00:50:45.409
You know,

00:50:47.144 --> 00:50:49.946 align:start size:94% position:6%
Barbara Bush had a
saying that in a marriage

00:50:49.946 --> 00:50:53.550 align:start position:0%
both people needed to be willing
to go 60% of the way there.

00:50:54.951 --> 00:50:57.954
And I don't know if in
our public discourse,

00:50:57.954 --> 00:51:00.057
and in our public
policy debates,

00:51:00.057 --> 00:51:01.291
both sides aren't there yet.

00:51:01.291 --> 00:51:03.627
They're not willing to
go 60% of the way there.

00:51:03.627 --> 00:51:06.897
And I don't know what will
help us get to that point.

00:51:06.897 --> 00:51:09.633
I don't know what
the issue will be

00:51:09.633 --> 00:51:11.501
that will help get us there.

00:51:11.501 --> 00:51:12.335
- Yeah.

00:51:12.335 --> 00:51:13.804
I mean, unfortunately,

00:51:13.804 --> 00:51:16.306 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's almost like it has to
sort of be something terrible.

00:51:16.306 --> 00:51:19.142
Like, that's the only way we
could kind of come together.

00:51:19.142 --> 00:51:21.378 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like the way New York
was after nine 11, like--

00:51:21.378 --> 00:51:24.414 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, 'cause even the
pandemic didn't really do it.

00:51:24.414 --> 00:51:25.549
- No.

00:51:25.549 --> 00:51:26.917 align:start size:88% position:13%
If anything, it
exacerbated this stuff.

00:51:26.917 --> 00:51:28.852
Let's talk about the
last chapter though.

00:51:28.852 --> 00:51:32.189
I feel like that would
be a good way to wrap.

00:51:32.189 --> 00:51:33.290
It's called 'Serenity:

00:51:33.290 --> 00:51:35.959
how can you detox, recover,

00:51:35.959 --> 00:51:37.294
and find peace?'

00:51:37.294 --> 00:51:39.429 align:start size:81% position:19%
I feel like pretty
much everyone's

00:51:39.429 --> 00:51:41.531 align:start size:88% position:13%
looking to find a
little peace these days.

00:51:41.531 --> 00:51:43.033
That, you know,

00:51:43.033 --> 00:51:46.303
we're home more,
we're online more,

00:51:46.303 --> 00:51:49.706
social media is not
making us social.

00:51:49.706 --> 00:51:52.175 align:start size:88% position:13%
The quest for peace
doesn't seem to be going

00:51:52.175 --> 00:51:54.344
that well for a lot
of people right now.

00:51:54.344 --> 00:51:56.513
- Well, so I wrote that chapter

00:51:56.513 --> 00:52:01.184
because the serenity prayer
was prominent in my home

00:52:01.184 --> 00:52:03.019
growing up as part of
the Lutheran church.

00:52:03.019 --> 00:52:05.222 align:start size:84% position:16%
But also my mom went
through a program

00:52:05.222 --> 00:52:06.823
called Children of Alcoholics.

00:52:06.823 --> 00:52:09.025
So that's part of the AA system,

00:52:09.025 --> 00:52:12.195
and the serenity prayer is
very important for recovery.

00:52:13.330 --> 00:52:14.431 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I've known a
few people that have

00:52:14.431 --> 00:52:17.067 align:start size:84% position:16%
gone through
Alcoholics Anonymous.

00:52:17.067 --> 00:52:18.268
Bob Beckel,

00:52:18.268 --> 00:52:20.203
one of the first people
on The Five, he did,

00:52:20.203 --> 00:52:23.306
and it really helped
turn his life around.

00:52:23.306 --> 00:52:25.108
When I think about serenity,

00:52:26.109 --> 00:52:27.277
do you know those moments, Dave,

00:52:27.277 --> 00:52:28.712
in your life,

00:52:28.712 --> 00:52:30.714 align:start size:84% position:16%
and they're usually
just glimpses,

00:52:30.714 --> 00:52:33.550
when you feel like
you're right exactly

00:52:33.550 --> 00:52:36.453
where you're supposed
to be at the moment.

00:52:36.453 --> 00:52:38.255
- They're rare and
they're fleeting.

00:52:38.255 --> 00:52:39.990
- It's such a gift.

00:52:39.990 --> 00:52:42.659
And I feel like that's
what God's intention was,

00:52:42.659 --> 00:52:45.095
for us to be able to
have more of that.

00:52:45.095 --> 00:52:46.730
And when I mentor
these young women,

00:52:46.730 --> 00:52:49.199 align:start size:84% position:16%
I find that they're
nowhere close

00:52:49.199 --> 00:52:50.600
to being able to find that.

00:52:50.600 --> 00:52:52.869 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I have lots of
different suggestions

00:52:52.869 --> 00:52:55.205
in that chapter for how
somebody might find it.

00:52:55.205 --> 00:52:57.307 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's just some things
that work for me.

00:52:58.975 --> 00:53:00.143
I write a lot of lists,

00:53:01.778 --> 00:53:03.880
and if I feel like I'm
being concerned with,

00:53:03.880 --> 00:53:06.516
consumed with worry or anxiety,

00:53:06.516 --> 00:53:08.852
on a piece of paper I will write

00:53:08.852 --> 00:53:10.887
my list of concerns and worries,

00:53:10.887 --> 00:53:12.822
and then I'll make two columns.

00:53:12.822 --> 00:53:14.424
And that next column I'll say,

00:53:14.424 --> 00:53:17.160
is this something that I
have control over or not.

00:53:18.028 --> 00:53:19.296
Mark it.

00:53:19.296 --> 00:53:22.365
For the things that
I have control over,

00:53:22.365 --> 00:53:24.434 align:start size:88% position:13%
I then in the third
column make a little bit

00:53:24.434 --> 00:53:26.803 align:start size:88% position:13%
of a notation about what
I could do about it.

00:53:28.305 --> 00:53:29.906
And then I learned this exercise

00:53:29.906 --> 00:53:32.242
when I was in college
and pretty stressed.

00:53:32.242 --> 00:53:34.044
I take that piece of paper,

00:53:34.044 --> 00:53:36.479 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I carry it with
me in my purse,

00:53:36.479 --> 00:53:37.814
in my backpack, my back pocket,

00:53:37.814 --> 00:53:39.282
wherever it might be,

00:53:39.282 --> 00:53:41.751 align:start size:97% position:3%
so that when I feel that
sort of anxiety bubbling up,

00:53:41.751 --> 00:53:43.920
I can pull that back
out and look at it,

00:53:43.920 --> 00:53:44.754
and know, okay,

00:53:44.754 --> 00:53:45.589
wait, wait.

00:53:45.589 --> 00:53:47.090
Okay.

00:53:47.090 --> 00:53:49.025
I've got a plan here for how
to deal with those things.

00:53:49.025 --> 00:53:51.628 align:start size:84% position:16%
I've also found that
guided meditation

00:53:51.628 --> 00:53:54.331
for me has been very useful.

00:53:54.331 --> 00:53:55.832 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then I would
say one other thing,

00:53:55.832 --> 00:53:57.167
which is, as you said,

00:53:57.167 --> 00:54:00.036
we're quite isolated
and we're on the Zooms.

00:54:00.036 --> 00:54:01.338 align:start size:84% position:16%
And even though I
love the connection,

00:54:01.338 --> 00:54:02.539
I love being able to talk to you

00:54:02.539 --> 00:54:04.674 align:start size:84% position:16%
and to see you like
in the Jetsons.

00:54:04.674 --> 00:54:06.009 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Yeah.
(laughs quickly)

00:54:06.009 --> 00:54:07.410
No, well that's the irony of it,

00:54:07.410 --> 00:54:09.179
right? It's like, I
didn't even know you.

00:54:09.179 --> 00:54:10.614
I don't think I had ever,

00:54:10.614 --> 00:54:11.848 align:start size:94% position:6%
I knew who you were
obviously for many years,

00:54:11.848 --> 00:54:14.184
but I don't think I had
done your show until,

00:54:14.184 --> 00:54:16.186
no, maybe I, no, we did
do it in person once,

00:54:16.186 --> 00:54:17.420
a little bit before lockdown--

00:54:17.420 --> 00:54:18.955
- You were in town,
you were in the city.

00:54:18.955 --> 00:54:20.390
- But in essence, we became
friends through this thing,

00:54:20.390 --> 00:54:21.958
which is a very weird, you know.

00:54:21.958 --> 00:54:23.793
We've never had
a drink together.

00:54:23.793 --> 00:54:24.628
We've never had
a meal together--

00:54:24.628 --> 00:54:25.695
- Oh, let's fix that.

00:54:27.097 --> 00:54:29.499 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you know what would
really help in Washington?

00:54:29.499 --> 00:54:32.502
I feel like everyone needs
to go and get drunk together,

00:54:33.970 --> 00:54:35.338
with no phones.

00:54:35.338 --> 00:54:36.673
But like, imagine,

00:54:36.673 --> 00:54:38.141
you know, you could imagine,

00:54:38.141 --> 00:54:39.476
you know, Ted Cruz getting drunk

00:54:39.476 --> 00:54:41.111
with Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez,

00:54:41.111 --> 00:54:42.345 align:start size:84% position:16%
they'd probably
strike up a friendship

00:54:42.345 --> 00:54:44.948
and maybe be able to
get to that unity.

00:54:44.948 --> 00:54:46.449
But anyway,

00:54:46.449 --> 00:54:47.884 align:start size:97% position:3%
the other thing that I started
doing a couple of years ago

00:54:47.884 --> 00:54:50.220 align:start size:84% position:16%
was an exercise to get
out of my own mind,

00:54:50.220 --> 00:54:52.622
because something that I
remind people of here is

00:54:52.622 --> 00:54:55.091 align:start position:0%
just when you think
everybody's thinking about you,

00:54:55.091 --> 00:54:57.193 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you think that they're
thinking negatively about you,

00:54:57.193 --> 00:55:00.463
you have to remember no
one's thinking about you.

00:55:00.463 --> 00:55:02.632
They are thinking
about themselves.

00:55:02.632 --> 00:55:04.234
- Yeah, yeah.

00:55:04.234 --> 00:55:06.703 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And also I remember
everybody's going
through something.

00:55:06.703 --> 00:55:07.604
Everybody.

00:55:07.604 --> 00:55:08.538
Look, Dave,

00:55:08.538 --> 00:55:09.406
you're going through something.

00:55:10.573 --> 00:55:13.209
My sister's friend,
she is going through,

00:55:13.209 --> 00:55:15.011 align:start size:81% position:19%
she just had a
double mastectomy.

00:55:15.011 --> 00:55:17.547
Thankfully it looks like
cancer free, you know.

00:55:17.547 --> 00:55:18.815
Peter and I are going
through something.

00:55:18.815 --> 00:55:20.617
Everybody's going
through something.

00:55:20.617 --> 00:55:22.252
So what I started doing is,

00:55:22.252 --> 00:55:26.389
anybody that I see
that I'm walking by,

00:55:26.389 --> 00:55:28.792
or on the subway when I
used to take the subway,

00:55:29.959 --> 00:55:31.995 align:start size:84% position:16%
people that I won't
necessarily say hi to,

00:55:31.995 --> 00:55:33.997 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'll just say a little
prayer for them.

00:55:33.997 --> 00:55:38.034
So like, I remember this
one lady and her daughter.

00:55:38.034 --> 00:55:40.170
They were doing homework on
the subway on the way home.

00:55:40.170 --> 00:55:42.072
And I remember, like,

00:55:42.072 --> 00:55:44.007
please help them
get home safely.

00:55:44.908 --> 00:55:46.509
To the guy, I hope that he has

00:55:46.509 --> 00:55:48.278
a really productive day at work.

00:55:48.278 --> 00:55:50.347 align:start size:97% position:3%
I hope that she is treated
well by all of her colleagues.

00:55:50.347 --> 00:55:52.482
I hope nobody, the guy
that works in retail,

00:55:52.482 --> 00:55:54.217 align:start size:84% position:16%
I can't stand it
when anybody is mean

00:55:54.217 --> 00:55:56.319
to anybody that works
in customer service.

00:55:56.319 --> 00:55:58.822
And really like, I pray that
nobody's bad to him today,

00:55:58.822 --> 00:56:01.024
or like negative
towards him today.

00:56:01.024 --> 00:56:04.828 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it kind of
became a habit that,

00:56:04.828 --> 00:56:07.664 align:start size:84% position:16%
it really helps me get
out of my own head,

00:56:07.664 --> 00:56:09.132
get out of my own way,

00:56:09.132 --> 00:56:12.335 align:start size:84% position:16%
and to sort of
spread this kindness,

00:56:12.335 --> 00:56:15.271 align:start position:0%
because I really think that
that's the way to reduce anxiety

00:56:15.271 --> 00:56:17.841 align:start size:94% position:6%
and increase your ability
to be your full self

00:56:17.841 --> 00:56:19.476
that will hopefully open up

00:56:19.476 --> 00:56:22.011 align:start size:84% position:16%
opportunities and
doors for promotion,

00:56:22.011 --> 00:56:23.780
raises, a change in career,

00:56:23.780 --> 00:56:27.550
or even if you haven't met
that lucky somebody yet,

00:56:27.550 --> 00:56:30.053
open your heart up to meeting

00:56:30.053 --> 00:56:31.688 align:start size:84% position:16%
somebody and making
a commitment.

00:56:32.555 --> 00:56:33.490
- Well, Dana,

00:56:33.490 --> 00:56:34.924
that's the perfect ending,

00:56:34.924 --> 00:56:38.728 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I have to return the
favor that you often offer me,

00:56:38.728 --> 00:56:40.663 align:start size:84% position:16%
which is to bring
Clyde on your show--

00:56:40.663 --> 00:56:42.065
- Oh, that's Sam.

00:56:42.065 --> 00:56:43.066
- No, no,

00:56:43.066 --> 00:56:44.134
I don't need to bring Clyde on.

00:56:44.134 --> 00:56:45.301
This is my show.

00:56:45.301 --> 00:56:46.703
Is Jasper around, or?

00:56:46.703 --> 00:56:48.204
- No, I'm at work.

00:56:48.204 --> 00:56:50.573 align:start size:84% position:16%
But hold on, I'm going
to get something.

00:56:50.573 --> 00:56:51.608
Hold on.

00:56:51.608 --> 00:56:53.076
(laughing)

00:56:53.076 --> 00:56:57.781
So I went on that book
tour with my Jasper book,

00:56:58.648 --> 00:56:59.682
and I bought one of these.

00:57:00.650 --> 00:57:01.451
This is--

00:57:01.451 --> 00:57:02.685
- Oh, there you go.

00:57:02.685 --> 00:57:04.754
- This is felt Jasper.

00:57:04.754 --> 00:57:06.256
You gotta get a few of these.

00:57:06.256 --> 00:57:08.725
Get a felt Clyde, oh.

00:57:08.725 --> 00:57:10.160
They're the best toys.

00:57:10.160 --> 00:57:12.228 align:start size:94% position:6%
Only thing is Jasper
really wants to eat this.

00:57:13.430 --> 00:57:14.964
- Well, Jasper should be
allowed to eat Jasper--

00:57:14.964 --> 00:57:16.099
- He wants this toy.

00:57:16.099 --> 00:57:17.700
They're kind of expensive,

00:57:17.700 --> 00:57:19.102
but if you go on Etsy,

00:57:19.102 --> 00:57:20.737
you can send them a
picture of your dog

00:57:20.737 --> 00:57:22.105
and they'll make you one.

00:57:22.105 --> 00:57:24.274
Even has like a little ear.

00:57:25.775 --> 00:57:27.377
- What a world, what a world.

00:57:27.377 --> 00:57:29.279
- Dave, you're a great American,

00:57:29.279 --> 00:57:30.613
and a wonderful person.

00:57:30.613 --> 00:57:31.448
Thank you.

00:57:32.615 --> 00:57:33.850
- Right back at you.

00:57:33.850 --> 00:57:35.552 align:start size:84% position:16%
The book is Everything
Will Be Okay,

00:57:35.552 --> 00:57:37.053
and everything will be okay.

00:57:37.053 --> 00:57:38.688
It really, it really will be.

00:57:38.688 --> 00:57:40.156
- It will be.

00:57:40.156 --> 00:57:43.026
- I look forward to seeing
you in person and good luck--

00:57:43.026 --> 00:57:44.961 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Catch a drink.
- I was about to say good luck

00:57:44.961 --> 00:57:46.129
with the rest of the book tour,

00:57:46.129 --> 00:57:47.664 align:start size:84% position:16%
but good luck
sitting in that seat

00:57:47.664 --> 00:57:49.599 align:start size:88% position:13%
talking to other people
like me I suppose.

00:57:49.599 --> 00:57:50.767
- Thanks Dave.

00:57:50.767 --> 00:57:52.235
